[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/a050a42e-e001-40cb-a21a-b5ad2c44c2d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. You will face this 18% of the time, and it was played in almost 660 million games! The move ...c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4-square. In this course, we're going to play a3 to prepare a gambit with b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6, and it's played over 40% of the time. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 73% of the time. We'll recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight and has now officially won a pawn. We'll attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat. The move 5.c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 almost half the time. This move is probably most natural, but it also allows us to have the most fun. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3! From a3 our knight has access to the b5- and c4-squares, which will be crucial in all of the following lines.} 8.Na3 8...e5 {The move ...e5 happens 26% of the time. Black wants to challenge the center right away, also taking control of the f4-square. We continue by jumping our knight into b5. This is our standard idea.} 9.Nb5 9...Bd6 {Black tries to use the fact that the e-pawn has moved in order to defend c7 with the bishop. We attack Black's queen with Bc4, another strong idea! The bishop on c4 is tactically defended since capturing it would lead to Nxd6+ with a fork of Black's king and queen.} 10.Bc4 10...Qxg2 {Black doesn't fear death and grabs the pawn on g2. We capture Black's undefended bishop with check.} 11.Nxd6+ 11...Kd7 {Black moves the king and attacks our knight.} 12.Ba3 {We defend the knight with Ba3 and are completely winning. If Black were to take our rook, we would crush their dreams with Qg4+. Otherwise, we can simply continue with Qf3 and be up a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/a050a42e-e001-40cb-a21a-b5ad2c44c2d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. You will face this 18% of the time, and it was played in almost 660 million games! The move ...c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4-square. In this course, we're going to play a3 to prepare a gambit with b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6, and it's played over 40% of the time. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 73% of the time. We'll recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight and has now officially won a pawn. We'll attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat. The move 5.c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 almost half the time. This move is probably most natural, but it also allows us to have the most fun. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3! From a3 our knight has access to the b5- and c4-squares, which will be crucial in all of the following lines.} 8.Na3 8...e5 {The move ...e5 happens 26% of the time. Black wants to challenge the center right away, also taking control of the f4-square. We continue by jumping our knight into b5. This is our standard idea.} 9.Nb5 9...Bd6 {Black tries to use the fact that the e-pawn has moved in order to defend c7 with the bishop. We attack Black's queen with Bc4, another strong idea! The bishop on c4 is tactically defended since capturing it would lead to Nxd6+ with a fork of Black's king and queen.} 10.Bc4 10...Qe4+ {Black moves the queen and gives a check on e4. We block the check with the knight. Black now cannot capture the g2-pawn, since we would capture Black's undefended d6-bishop with check and follow it up with Rg1.} 11.Ne2 11...Bb8 {Black retreats the undefended bishop to b8, still covering the c7-square. Since our bishop is already on c4, we can use this time to pressure another weakness. So we develop the queen to b3 and attack the f7-pawn.} 12.Qb3 12...Qg6 {Black retreats the queen to g6 and defends f7.} 13.O-O {We simply castle our king. White now has a winning advantage. Black is horribly underdeveloped and we will combine the ideas of d5, Ba3, f4 and Ng3 in order to cause Black further headaches.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 5"]
