oves the queen and gives a check on e4. We block the check with the knight. Black now cannot capture the g2-pawn, since we would capture Black's undefended d6-bishop with check and follow it up with Rg1.} 11.Ne2 11...Bb8 {Black retreats the undefended bishop to b8, still covering the c7-square. Since our bishop is already on c4, we can use this time to pressure another weakness. So we develop the queen to b3 and attack the f7-pawn.} 12.Qb3 12...Nd8 {Black can also defend f7 by giving up all hope and retreating their only developed minor piece to the back rank.} 13.Ba3 {We continue with Ba3. White is completely winning. We will continue by castling our king and Black will struggle to get their king to safety. Black's pieces simply look sad.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/a050a42e-e001-40cb-a21a-b5ad2c44c2d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. You will face this 18% of the time, and it was played in almost 660 million games! The move ...c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4-square. In this course, we're going to play a3 to prepare a gambit with b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6, and it's played over 40% of the time. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 73% of the time. We'll recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight and has now officially won a pawn. We'll attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat. The move 5.c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 almost half the time. This move is probably most natural, but it also allows us to have the most fun. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3! From a3 our knight has access to the b5- and c4-squares, which will be crucial in all of the following lines.} 8.Na3 8...e6 {...e6 is third in popularity and is equally as bad as ...Nf6 and ...e5. You will face it 18% of the time. We continue by jumping our knight into b5 and threatening a "family fork" on c7.} 9.Nb5 9...Bd6 {Black tries to use the fact that the e-pawn has moved in order to defend c7 with the bishop.} 10.Qg4 {Don't blindly rush with Bc4. There is a notable difference between ...e5 and ...e6. Instead, we play the crushing Qg4! We are threatening both the g7-pawn and Bc4 to trap Black's queen! Black has no way to defend against both threats and White is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/a050a42e-e001-40cb-a21a-b5ad2c44c2d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. You will face this 18% of the time, and it was played in almost 660 million games! The move ...c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4-square. In this course, we're going to play a3 to prepare a gambit with b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6, and it's played over 40% of the time. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 73% of the time. We'll recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight and has now officially won a pawn. We'll attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat. The move 5.c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 almost half the time. This move is probably most natural, but it also allows us to have the most fun. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3! From a3 our knight has access to the b5- and c4-squares, which will be crucial in all of the following lines.} 8.Na3 8...Nf6 {...Nf6 is very natural and happens 23% of the time, but it pretty much loses on the spot. Black won't be ready for the aggression that's about to come. We continue by jumping our knight into b5, threatening a fork on c7.} 9.Nb5 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nc7 by retreating the queen to d8. We continue the initiative by attacking Black's knight with d5! One of our main ideas is to develop the bishop to f4 in order to support Nc7+.} 10.d5 10...Ne5 {Black moves the knight to e5. We attack the knight again by developing our bishop to f4! This gives us a tempo and forces Black to become even more passive.} 11.Bf4 11...Nfd7 {Black can't move the knight because that would give us access to the c7-square. Instead, Black's only try is to defend the knight with ...Nfd7. We attack the knight one more time with Nf3!} 12.Nf3 12...f6 {Black is desperate and defends the knight once more with ...f6. But we jump our f3-knight into d4, taking advantage of the light-squared weakness on e6! Our threat is now Ne6, which intends to either fork Black with Nc7+ or to, in conjunction with Be3, trap Black's queen.} 13.Nfd4 13...Qb6 {Black might also move their queen to b6.} 14.Ne6 {We jump the knight into e6 with the aforementioned threats. Black simply has no defense. Our aggression on the e5-knight has provoked Black to make more weaknesses, which we exploited immediately.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/a050a42e-e001-40cb-a21a-b5ad2c44c2d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. You will face this 18% of the time, and it was played in almost 660 million games! The move ...c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4-square. In this course, we're going to play a3 to prepare a gambit with b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6, and it's played over 40% of the time. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 73% of the time. We'll recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight and has now officially won a pawn. We'll attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat. The move 5.c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 almost half the time. This move is probably most natural, but it also allows us to have the most fun. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3! From a3 our knight has access to the b5- and c4-squares, which will be crucial in all of the following lines.} 8.Na3 8...Nf6 {...Nf6 is very natural and happens 23% of the time, but it pretty much loses on the spot. Black won't be ready for the aggression that's about to come. We continue by jumping our knight into b5, threatening a fork on c7.} 9.Nb5 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nc7 by retreating the queen to d8. We continue the initiative by attacking Black's knight with d5! One of our main ideas is to develop the bishop to f4 in order to support Nc7+.} 10.d5 10...Ne5 {Black moves the knight to e5. We attack the knight again by developing our bishop to f4! This gives us a tempo and forces Black to become even more passive.} 11.Bf4 11...Nfd7 {Black can't move the knight because that would give us access to the c7-square. Instead, Black's only try is to defend the knight with ...Nfd7. We attack the knight one more time with Nf3!} 12.Nf3 12...f6 {Black is desperate and defends the knight once more with ...f6. But we jump our f3-knight into d4, taking advantage of the light-squared weakness on e6! Our threat is now Ne6, which intends to either fork Black with Nc7+ or to, in conjunction with Be3, trap Black's queen.} 13.Nfd4 13...Nb6 {...Nb6 is most common. But it allows to remove one of the defenders now and go for another key idea. We continue by capturing the e5-knight.} 14.Bxe5 14...fxe5 {Black recaptures on e5. We'll play the crushing Qh5+! This is another move made possible by Black's ...f6 push.} 15.Qh5+ 15...g6 {Black blocks the check with ...g6.} 16.Qxe5 {We capture on the pawn on e5. Black is simply dead lost as there is no way for them to defend against the combined threats of Qxa8 and Nc7+.} *

