t again by developing our bishop to f4! This gives us a tempo and forces Black to become even more passive.} 11.Bf4 11...Nfd7 {Black can't move the knight because that would give us access to the c7-square. Instead, Black's only try is to defend the knight with ...Nfd7. We attack the knight one more time with Nf3!} 12.Nf3 12...f6 {Black is desperate and defends the knight once more with ...f6. But we jump our f3-knight into d4, taking advantage of the light-squared weakness on e6! Our threat is now Ne6, which intends to either fork Black with Nc7+ or to, in conjunction with Be3, trap Black's queen.} 13.Nfd4 13...Nc5 {...Nc5 is second most common but leads to the same thing as ...Nb6. We continue by capturing the e5-knight.} 14.Bxe5 14...fxe5 {Black recaptures on e5. We play the crushing Qh5+! Black should probably resign, but can also play on and prolong their suffering.} 15.Qh5+ 15...g6 {Black blocks the check with ...g6.} 16.Qxe5 {We capture on the pawn on e5. Black can't defend against both threats and we are winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/a050a42e-e001-40cb-a21a-b5ad2c44c2d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. You will face this 18% of the time, and it was played in almost 660 million games! The move ...c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4-square. In this course, we're going to play a3 to prepare a gambit with b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6, and it's played over 40% of the time. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 73% of the time. We'll recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight and has now officially won a pawn. We'll attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat. The move 5.c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 almost half the time. This move is probably most natural, but it also allows us to have the most fun. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3! From a3 our knight has access to the b5- and c4-squares, which will be crucial in all of the following lines.} 8.Na3 8...e5 {The move ...e5 happens 26% of the time. Black wants to challenge the center right away, also taking control of the f4-square. We continue by jumping our knight into b5. This is our standard idea.} 9.Nb5 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nc7 by retreating the queen to d8. We continue the initiative by attacking Black's knight with d5! SInce we can't play Bf4, our main idea is to march the pawn down to d6.} 10.d5 10...Nb8 {Black retreats the knight and sets up for the next game with ...Nb8. We continue with our plan by playing d6, adjusting to Black's play. Since they committed with ...e5, the d-pawn now has no adversaries in its path.} 11.d6 11...Na6 {Black desperately tries to hold on and defend c7 with ...Na6. But we strike with Rxa6!, sacrificing the exchange and removing the defender of the c7-square. A pretty logical tactic.} 12.Rxa6 12...bxa6 {Black recaptures on a6. We'll jump in with Nc7+! This is a deadly combination.} 13.Nc7+ 13...Kd7 {Black moves the king to d7 as to avoid giving up their queen. Don't rush to capture the rook on a8! A much stronger response is Qa4+, which initiates a crushing attack on Black's king.} 14.Qa4+ 14...Kxd6 {Black plays the only legal move and captures on d6.} 15.Ne8+ {We continue the attack with Ne8+. Our bishops will quickly develop to a3 and c4 and Black's king won't be able to find any shelter. White is completely winning, the evaluation is astronomically high, and Black's best option is to give up their queen with ...Qxe8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 8"]
