rs whatsoever.} 11.d6 {We continue with our plan by playing d6. White is completely winning. The c7-square is now controlled by our pawn and Nc7+ is impossible to prevent.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 1: Bad Responses After Black Plays 6...d5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/a050a42e-e001-40cb-a21a-b5ad2c44c2d9/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. You will face this 18% of the time, and it was played in almost 660 million games! The move ...c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4-square. In this course, we're going to play a3 to prepare a gambit with b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6, and it's played over 40% of the time. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 73% of the time. We'll recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight and has now officially won a pawn. We'll attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat. The move 5.c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 almost half the time. This move is probably most natural, but it also allows us to have the most fun. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3! From a3 our knight has access to the b5- and c4-squares, which will be crucial in all of the following lines.} 8.Na3 8...e6 {...e6 is third in popularity and is equally as bad as ...Nf6 and ...e5. You will face it 18% of the time. We continue by jumping our knight into b5 and threatening a "family fork" on c7.} 9.Nb5 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nc7 by retreating the queen to d8. This is most common. Don't rush with Bf4. Instead, we continue with d5! The main idea of this clearance sacrifice is to remove Black's pawn from the e-file, so that Bf4 can no longer be met with ...e5.} 10.d5 10...exd5 {Black captures the d5-pawn.} 11.Bf4 {We develop our bishop to f4 and support the c7-square. Black doesn't really have a convenient way to prevent Nc7+ and White simply has a winning position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...a6 {Black responds with ...a6. This move controls the b5-square and it's played 14% of the time. In a complicated sequence of moves, Nb5 isn't the best choice for White now. We continue by jumping our knight into c4, threatening a fork on b6 instead.} 9.Nc4 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nb6 by retreating the queen to d8. We continue the initiative by attacking Black's knight with d5! As you can see, d5 is often a hidden and strong idea.} 10.d5 10...Na7 {Black retreats the knight to a7. We'll continue with Be3, supporting the b6-square for our knight.} 11.Be3 11...b5 {Black has to play energetically with ...b5. This move is important since it will allow Black to attack and remove our knight when it jumps into b6. We continue by jumping our knight into b6 and attacking Black's rook and bishop anyway.} 12.Nb6 12...Rb8 {Black moves the rook and attacks our knight. We'll capture the bishop on c8.} 13.Nxc8 13...Nxc8 {Black recaptures with the knight, and this is the only move which doesn't immediately lose.} 14.Rxa6 {We capture the a6-pawn. The material is now equal, but White has a very pleasant advantage. We can develop pretty flexibly and the general ideas include Bf4, Nf3 and Qb3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...a6 {Black responds with ...a6. This move controls the b5-square and it's played 14% of the time. In a complicated sequence of moves, Nb5 isn't the best choice for White now. We continue by jumping our knight into c4, threatening a fork on b6 instead.} 9.Nc4 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nb6 by retreating the queen to d8. We continue the initiative by attacking Black's knight with d5! As you can see, d5 is often a hidden and strong idea.} 10.d5 10...Na7 {Black retreats the knight to a7. We'll continue with Be3, supporting the b6-square for our knight.} 11.Be3 11...b5 {Black has to play energetically with ...b5. This move is important since it will allow Black to attack and remove our knight when it jumps into b6. We continue by jumping our knight into b6 and attacking Black's rook and bishop anyway.} 12.Nb6 12...Rb8 {Black moves the rook and attacks our knight. We'll capture the bishop on c8.} 13.Nxc8 13...Qxc8 {This is natural but losing. Black recaptures with the queen, defending the a-pawn and counterattacking the c-pawn.} 14.Qb3 {We simply defend the c-pawn with Qb3. We will follow up with by capturing Black's knight on a7 and the b5-pawn will fall due to an eventual Bxb5+. According to the engine, White has a +6 advantage in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...Bf5 {Black goes for ...Bf5 8% of the time. Black develops the light-squared bishop and, consequently, frees up the c8-square. We continue by jumping our knight into b5, threatening a "family fork" on c7.} 9.Nb5 9...Rc8 {Black defends c7 with the rook. This leaves the a-pawn hanging, but Black doesn't mind giving back the extra pawn in order to get some activity. We capture the a7-pawn.} 10.Nxa7 10...Nxa7 {Black captures the knight on a7. We recapture on a7.} 11.Rxa7 11...Nf6 {Black can also continue by naturally developing with ...Nf6.} 12.Nf3 {We can simply develop our knight to f3. The position is pretty pleasant and we will continue to develop with Qa4+, Be2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...Bf5 {Black goes for ...Bf5 8% of the time. Black develops the light-squared bishop and, consequently, frees up the c8-square. We continue by jumping our knight into b5, threatening a "family fork" on c7.} 9.Nb5 9...Rc8 {Black defends c7 with the rook. This leaves the a-pawn hanging, but Black doesn't mind giving back the extra pawn in order to get some activity. We capture the a7-pawn.} 10.Nxa7 10...Nxa7 {Black captures the knight on a7. We recapture on a7.} 11.Rxa7 11...e5 {Most direct is ...e5. Black expands in the center and immediately challenges our central structure. We respond with Nf3, developing a piece and putting pressure on the e-pawn.} 12.Nf3 12...exd4 {Black quickly releases the tension and captures on d4. This position occured between Carlsen and Inarkiev in 2017. The game was won by Magnus and continued Nxd4...} 13.Qxd4 {Magnus' move was okay, but we can still improve on his play with Qxd4. We want to trade queens and quickly develop with Bb5+ and castle. White has a pretty pleasant position and a modest advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...Qa5 {This is the critical move! Black prevents our knight from moving and puts pressure on the c-pawn. ...Qa5 is only played 1% of the time, but you need to know how to meet the critical response. The sneaky Nf3 tactically defends the c-pawn, since ...Qxc3+ is met with Bd2, trapping Black's queen.} 9.Nf3 9...Nf6 {Black can also continue by developing with ...Nf6. We develop our bishop to f4 and support the c7-square. We are now threatening Nb5, which would open up an attack on Black's queen and threaten a fork on c7 at the same time.} 10.Bf4 10...Qd8 {Black steps off the a-file by retreating the queen to d8. We continue by developing the light-squared bishop to c4. We are now threatening Nb5, followed by the unstoppable Nc7+. The usual ideas.} 11.Bc4 11...a6 {Black plays ...a6 and prevents Nb5. Another common idea is to push the d-pawn when we have the space and disorganize Black's pieces. We attack Black's knight with d5. The pawn on d5 also serves as a guarantee that Black can't play ...e5.} 12.d5 12...Na5 {Black jumps the knight out to a5 and attacks our bishop, but we have a tactic ready. We respond with the shocking Bb5+! The bishop cannot be captured since after ...axb5 Nxb5, Black has no good way of dealing with the threat of Nc7+.} 13.Bb5+ 13...Bd7 {Instead, Black blocks the check with the bishop and offers a trade. We capture the bishop on d7.} 14.Bxd7+ 14...Qxd7 {Black recaptures on d7.} 15.c4 {We continue by supporting the d-pawn with c4. Our position is very pleasant and we will continue to improve it with O-O and Ne5. Even with the most careful play, Black is still slightly worse.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...Qa5 {This is the critical move! Black prevents our knight from moving and puts pressure on the c-pawn. ...Qa5 is only played 1% of the time, but you need to know how to meet the critical response. The sneaky Nf3 tactically defends the c-pawn, since ...Qxc3+ is met with Bd2, trapping Black's queen.} 9.Nf3 9...Nf6 {Black can also continue by developing with ...Nf6. We develop our bishop to f4 and support the c7-square. We are now threatening Nb5, which would open up an attack on Black's queen and threaten a fork on c7 at the same time.} 10.Bf4 10...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5 and attacks our c-pawn and bishop.} 11.Bd2 {We respond by retreating the bishop back to d2. The c-pawn cannot be captured as after ...Nxc3 Qc1, Black loses a piece. We will continue to expand with c4 and follow it up with either c5 or d5. We'll develop the bishop to e2 and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...a6 {Black responds with ...a6. This move controls the b5-square and it's played 14% of the time. In a complicated sequence of moves, Nb5 isn't the best choice for White now. We continue by jumping our knight into c4, threatening a fork on b6 instead.} 9.Nc4 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nb6 by retreating the queen to d8. We continue the initiative by attacking Black's knight with d5! As you can see, d5 is often a hidden and strong idea.} 10.d5 10...Nb8 {Most common is ...Nb8: Black retreats the knight to the home square. We move the knight once again and jump it into e5! The main idea behind this move is to prepare Bb5+ and to put some pressure on the f7-pawn. We are flexible and easily change between the common targets.} 11.Ne5 11...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, developing a piece and attacking our d-pawn. We'll continue with our plan and give a check with Bb5+.} 12.Bb5+ 12...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop and offers a trade.} 13.Bc4 {We simply decline the trade by retreating the bishop to c4. White has a comfortable advantage in this position. Our development plan is Nf3 with O-O and there are many interesting tactical ideas involving a timely d6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...a6 {Black responds with ...a6. This move controls the b5-square and it's played 14% of the time. In a complicated sequence of moves, Nb5 isn't the best choice for White now. We continue by jumping our knight into c4, threatening a fork on b6 instead.} 9.Nc4 9...Qd8 {Black defends against Nb6 by retreating the queen to d8. We continue the initiative by attacking Black's knight with d5! As you can see, d5 is often a hidden and strong idea.} 10.d5 10...b5 {Black tries to solve their problems tactically by counterattacking our knight with ...b5. We'll take on c6.} 11.dxc6 11...Qxd1+ {Black throws in an "intermezzo" and trades queens on d1, taking away our right to castle. We play the only legal move and recapture on d1.} 12.Kxd1 12...bxc4 {Black captures the knight on c4.} 13.Nf3 {We don't need to rush with Bxc4! We develop our knight to f3 first in order to prevent Black from expanding with ...e5. White has a comfortable advantage as we will be able to win the c4-pawn. Black's a-pawn will remain weak.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...Bf5 {Black goes for ...Bf5 8% of the time. Black develops the light-squared bishop and, consequently, frees up the c8-square. We continue by jumping our knight into b5, threatening a "family fork" on c7.} 9.Nb5 9...Qd8 {Black can also defend against Nc7 by retreating the queen to d8. As we've seen in the previous study, this really isn't something Black should be doing. We'll attack the knight with d5, also preparing Bf4 to support the c7-square.} 10.d5 10...Ne5 {Black moves the knight to e5. We attack the knight again by developing our bishop to f4! If Black now moves the knight, we will simply win with Nc7+.} 11.Bf4 11...f6 {Black's only try is to defend the knight with ...f6. We attack the knight one more time with Nf3! If our knight is now captured, we can simply recapture with the queen and Black's queenside will quickly collapse.} 12.Nf3 12...a6 {Black forces our knight to move by attacking it with ...a6. We move the knight to d4 and attack Black's light-squared bishop.} 13.Nbd4 13...Nxf3+ {Black can try to prevent the loss of material with Nxf3+. We recapture on f3 with the queen.} 14.Qxf3 14...Bd7 {The bishop was attacked, so Black retreats it to d7.} 15.Bc4 {We simply continue by developing our light-squared bishop. White is already completely winning. We will castle our king and try to expose the horribly weak light squares in Black's position. Black is underdeveloped and the Black king won't get castled anytime soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...Qa5 {This is the critical move! Black prevents our knight from moving and puts pressure on the c-pawn. ...Qa5 is only played 1% of the time, but you need to know how to meet the critical response. The sneaky Nf3 tactically defends the c-pawn, since ...Qxc3+ is met with Bd2, trapping Black's queen.} 9.Nf3 9...e6 {Most common and objectively best is ...e6.} 10.Bb5 {We prepare to castle by developing the bishop to b5 and pinning Black's knight. The position is objectively equal. If Black is slow to castle and "wastes" a move on ...Bd7, we can prepare a d5-break with c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 2: Black Plays 6...d5, Solid Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/5945b203-6e69-4446-9de3-f9ed595142ca/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Once again, a3 for b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black wins a pawn on b4. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {You are, without question, going to face ...d5 the most. We continue by capturing on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the queen. We continue by developing our knight to the edge of the board with Na3!} 8.Na3 8...Qa5 {This is the critical move! Black prevents our knight from moving and puts pressure on the c-pawn. ...Qa5 is only played 1% of the time, but you need to know how to meet the critical response. The sneaky Nf3 tactically defends the c-pawn, since ...Qxc3+ is met with Bd2, trapping Black's queen.} 9.Nf3 9...Bg4 {Nearly equally as popular as ...e6 is ...Bg4. Black pins the knight and develops the bishop before playing ...e6. We respond with the fascinating novelty: Nc2, sacrificing the pawn! This move opens up an attack on Black's queen and aims to quickly centralize the knight with Ne3.} 10.Nc2 10...Qxc3+ {Black "calls the bluff" and captures the c3-pawn with check. We block the check and attack Black's queen with Bd2. The idea is to grab as many tempi as we can and improve after giving up the pawn.} 11.Bd2 11...Qb3 {Black moves the queen out of the attack. We continue by attacking Black's queen once more with Rb1. We will be targeting the b7-pawn as well.} 12.Rb1 12...Qe6+ {Black moves the queen to e6 and gives a check.} 13.Ne3 {We block the check with Ne3. Not only are we threatening to capture the b-pawn, but we are also threatening to win a piece with d5. Black doesn't really have a way to deal with both threats. Notable is that we always want to be meeting ...Bxf3 with gxf3!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...d5 {Black enters a French where there is no longer a ...c5 break, so we have a massive center and can expand. Expect to see this move more than 40% of the time. Instead of rushing with e5, we will proceed by supporting the e-pawn with Nbd2.} 8.Nbd2 8...dxe4 {Black immediately releases the tension and captures on e4. We recapture on e4 with the knight.} 9.Nxe4 9...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, which is natural but inaccurate. We continue by capturing the knight on f6. Black now probably doesn't want to capture with the g-pawn and ruin their kingside pawn structure.} 10.Nxf6+ 10...Qxf6 {So they recapture on f6 with the queen. However, the queen on f6 is a target and will help us obtain a significant lead in development. We develop our dark-squared bishop and attack Black's queen!} 11.Bg5 11...Qf5 {Black moves the queen to f5. We develop our light-squared bishop and attack Black's queen now!} 12.Bd3 12...Qd5 {Black moves the queen to d5. We proceed by castling our king.} 13.O-O 13...Be7 {Black offers a trade with ...Be7 and prepares to castle their king. We accept the trade and capture the bishop on e7. No point in retreating now.} 14.Bxe7 14...Nxe7 {Black recaptures on e7 with the knight.} 15.Qa4+ {We give a check on a4 with our queen. Depending on how Black blocks the check, our next move will either be Qa3 or Qb4, which will prevent Black from castling. White has a very pleasant position and it won't be easy for Black to untangle.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black inaccurately develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn a quarter of the time. This is not the best option because the knights in the center give us all the space in the world to push with tempo and make them targets. We'll respond energetically by attacking Black's knight with d5!} 8.d5 8...exd5 {Most common is ...exd5, but this is a mistake. We recapture on d5 with the e-pawn. Black's knight is under attack.} 9.exd5 9...Qe7+ {Black plays ...Qe7+. The primary idea of this move is to support the e5-square and prepare ...Ne5. We block the check by developing our light-squared bishop and preparing to castle.} 10.Be2 10...Ne5 {Black justifies their previous move and jumps the knight into e5. We continue by castling our king. We want to quickly place our rook on the open e-file, where three of Black's pieces are lined up.} 11.O-O 11...d6 {Black anticipates Re1 and supports the e5-knight with ...d6.} 12.Re1 {We continue by placing our rook on the open e-file. White now has a winning advantage and the position pretty much plays itself. We will put more pressure on the e5-knight with Bf4 and Black's position will quickly begin to collapse.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...d5 {Black enters a French where there is no longer a ...c5 break, so we have a massive center and can expand. Expect to see this move more than 40% of the time. Instead of rushing with e5, we will proceed by supporting the e-pawn with Nbd2.} 8.Nbd2 8...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and increases the pressure on the e-pawn. We still don't rush with e5! Instead, we support the e-pawn one more time by developing our light-squared bishop to d3.} 9.Bd3 9...dxe4 {Black releases the central tension with ...dxe4. We recapture on e4 with the knight.} 10.Nxe4 10...Be7 {Black continues developing with ...Be7 and prepares to castle their king. We'll trade on f6.} 11.Nxf6+ 11...Bxf6 {Black doesn't want to ruin their structure with ...gxf6 so they recapture with the bishop. We continue by castling our king.} 12.O-O 12...O-O {Black castles their king. At first glance, h4 just loses another pawn, but it is actually a very interesting dynamic sacrifice. If Black doesn't capture the pawn, we will continue the attack with Ng5.} 13.h4 13...Bxh4 {Black grabs the h4-pawn. We capture the bishop on h4.} 14.Nxh4 14...Qxh4 {Black recaptures on h4.} 15.Re1 {We continue with Re1. The main idea is that we want to attack Black by lifting our rook to either e3 or e4. We have the powerful bishops on d3 an c1 and our queen can quickly join the attack as well. In a position where we are down two pawns, the computer still gives a slight advantage to White.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...d5 {Black enters a French where there is no longer a ...c5 break, so we have a massive center and can expand. Expect to see this move more than 40% of the time. Instead of rushing with e5, we will proceed by supporting the e-pawn with Nbd2.} 8.Nbd2 8...dxe4 {Black immediately releases the tension and captures on e4. We recapture on e4 with the knight.} 9.Nxe4 9...Be7 {Black accurately continues by playing ...Be7 before developing with ...Nf6.} 10.Bd3 {We develop our light-squared bishop to d3. This position will transpose after 10...Nf6.} 10...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and we've transposed to 9...dxe4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...d5 {Black enters a French where there is no longer a ...c5 break, so we have a massive center and can expand. Expect to see this move more than 40% of the time. Instead of rushing with e5, we will proceed by supporting the e-pawn with Nbd2.} 8.Nbd2 8...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and increases the pressure on the e-pawn. We still don't rush with e5! Instead, we support the e-pawn one more time by developing our light-squared bishop to d3.} 9.Bd3 9...Be7 {Black continues developing with ...Be7 and prepares to castle their king.} 10.O-O {We simply castle our king. White has a minor edge in this position and we can continue to improve our position with e5 and Re1. One powerful attacking motif in these positions is Bb1, followed by Qc2 and h4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black inaccurately develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn a quarter of the time. This is not the best option because the knights in the center give us all the space in the world to push with tempo and make them targets. We'll respond energetically by attacking Black's knight with d5!} 8.d5 8...exd5 {Most common is ...exd5, but this is a mistake. We recapture on d5 with the e-pawn. Black's knight is under attack.} 9.exd5 9...Nb8 {Black retreats the knight to b8. We continue with the winning d6! Not only does this suffocate Black, but it also threatens the devastating Qe2+.} 10.d6 10...Qb6 {Black tries to solve their problems actively and attacks the d-pawn with ...Qb6. We play the crushing Qe2+, and Black must now move the king.} 11.Qe2+ 11...Kd8 {Black moves the king to d8.} 12.Ng5 {We continue with Ng5! There is no defense against the threat of Nxf7+, since the desperate attempt after ...Qb3 can be met with Ra3 and c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black inaccurately develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn a quarter of the time. This is not the best option because the knights in the center give us all the space in the world to push with tempo and make them targets. We'll respond energetically by attacking Black's knight with d5!} 8.d5 8...Nb8 {Black immediately retreats the knight to b8, which is the best move. We pin Black's knight with Bg5! One of our ideas is to attack the pinned knight with e5.} 9.Bg5 9...h6 {Black attacks our bishop with ...h6 and forces a decision. We will respond by capturing on f6.} 10.Bxf6 10...Qxf6 {Black recaptures with the queen in order to keep their pawn structure intact.} 11.e5 {We continue by attacking Black's queen with e5. Black's queen will move and we will follow up by "suffocating" our opponent with d6. One of our main ideas is to develop the knight to a3 in order to jump it into b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black inaccurately develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn a quarter of the time. This is not the best option because the knights in the center give us all the space in the world to push with tempo and make them targets. We'll respond energetically by attacking Black's knight with d5!} 8.d5 8...Nb8 {Black immediately retreats the knight to b8, which is the best move. We pin Black's knight with Bg5! One of our ideas is to attack the pinned knight with e5.} 9.Bg5 9...Be7 {The most common move and a horrible blunder is ...Be7. We continue by attacking Black's bishop with d6.} 10.d6 10...Bf8 {Black retreats the bishop to f8.} 11.Na3 {Don't rush with e5! Instead, we will continue by developing our knight to a3 from where it will be able to jump into b5. Black's position is hopeless and White is already completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black inaccurately develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn a quarter of the time. This is not the best option because the knights in the center give us all the space in the world to push with tempo and make them targets. We'll respond energetically by attacking Black's knight with d5!} 8.d5 8...Ne7 {Black can also retreat the knight to e7.} 9.d6 {We respond by attacking Black's knight with d6. Black can now move the knight to c6 or g6, after which we will establish a crushing positional bind with e5. White, of course, has a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Another option Black might go for is the non-committal ...d6, and they do so almost 10% of the time. We will continue by developing the light-squared bishop and pinning Black's knight. If Black ever attacks our bishop with ...a6, we will respond with Bxc6.} 8.Bb5 8...Bd7 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to d7 and unpins the knight. We continue by castling our king.} 9.O-O 9...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and attacks the e-pawn. We should ignore Black's attack and play the novelty, c4!, preparing d5 in the future. If Black now captures the pawn on e4, we will respond with Re1 and d5 and crash through on the e-file.} 10.c4 10...a6 {Black continues by attacking our bishop with ...a6. We capture the knight on c6.} 11.Bxc6 11...Bxc6 {Recapturing with the pawn is actually a mistake and would allow us to play Nc3, followed by e5. Here, we continue by attacking the bishop with d5, as this is how we want to be meeting Black's bishop whenever it arrives on c6. That's what c4 sets us up for, anyway.} 12.d5 12...Bd7 {Black retreats the bishop to d7.} 13.Nc3 {We continue by developing our knight to c3. According to the computer, the position is objectively equal. We are down a pawn, but we have sufficient dynamic compensation. Some of our ideas include Qb3, Ba3 and even a timely e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 3: Black Plays 6...e6 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/9e0e01a1-4427-4154-8f3d-1df9d56f8791/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play our b4-gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, opening the bishop on f8 and preparing a potential ...d5 expansion. This happens almost 30% of the time. We'll continue developing with the simple and strong Nf3.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Another option Black might go for is the non-committal ...d6, and they do so almost 10% of the time. We will continue by developing the light-squared bishop and pinning Black's knight. If Black ever attacks our bishop with ...a6, we will respond with Bxc6.} 8.Bb5 8...Bd7 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to d7 and unpins the knight. We continue by castling our king.} 9.O-O 9...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and attacks the e-pawn. We should ignore Black's attack and play the novelty, c4!, preparing d5 in the future. If Black now captures the pawn on e4, we will respond with Re1 and d5 and crash through on the e-file.} 10.c4 10...a6 {Black continues by attacking our bishop with ...a6. We capture the knight on c6.} 11.Bxc6 11...Bxc6 {Recapturing with the pawn is actually a mistake and would allow us to play Nc3, followed by e5. Here, we continue by attacking the bishop with d5, as this is how we want to be meeting Black's bishop whenever it arrives on c6. That's what c4 sets us up for, anyway.} 12.d5 12...exd5 {Black makes the mistake of capturing on d5. This will allow us to open the e-file and we will quickly punish Black for being underdeveloped. We recapture with the e-pawn as this opens the e-file.} 13.exd5 13...Bd7 {Black retreats the attacked bishop to the only safe square. We'll continue by giving a check with Re1+.} 14.Re1+ 14...Be7 {Black is forced to block with the bishop, since every other move loses material.} 15.Qe2 {We'll continue with Qe2, as this move prevents Black from castling. White is borderline winning and the main idea we will go for is Ra3, followed by Re3. Our b1-knight can develop to d2 and the dark-squared bishop can develop to b2.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black plays ...d6 12% of the time, controlling the e5-square and activating the c8-bishop. Black didn't do anything to prevent us from playing d5, so we should go for it and quickly begin an initiative. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black opts to jump the knight into e5. This is most common, but the knight on e5 is a target. We continue the initative by expanding in the center and attacking Black's knight with f4!} 8.f4 8...Nd7 {Black opts to retreat the knight to d7. We continue by developing our knight to f3. This move also supports a central break with e5.} 9.Nf3 9...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto their dark-squared bishop with ...g6. While this is the most common continuation, it is quite inaccurate. We respond with Qd4! This move exposes the weakened dark squares and forks Black's rook and the a7-pawn.} 10.Qd4 10...Ngf6 {Black defends the rook by developing the knight to f6. We march forward and attack Black's knight with e5! This is usually the case when we already have an extended structure and Black tries to develop with ...Nf6.} 11.e5 11...dxe5 {Black captures on e5. We'll recapture. Black's knight is now attacked and there is actually no way to prevent the loss of material.} 12.fxe5 12...Nh5 {Since retreating the knight would lose immediately to e6, Black might try ...Nh5. We'll respond by attacking Black's knight with g4!} 13.g4 13...Ng7 {Black retreats the knight to g7.} 14.Bb5 {We develop our bishop to b5 and pin Black's knight. The threat of e6 is now nearly impossible to prevent and White is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black plays ...d6 12% of the time, controlling the e5-square and activating the c8-bishop. Black didn't do anything to prevent us from playing d5, so we should go for it and quickly begin an initiative. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black opts to jump the knight into e5. This is most common, but the knight on e5 is a target. We continue the initative by expanding in the center and attacking Black's knight with f4!} 8.f4 8...Nd7 {Black opts to retreat the knight to d7. We continue by developing our knight to f3. This move also supports a central break with e5.} 9.Nf3 9...Ngf6 {The "other" move in this positon is ...Ngf6. Black develops their knight and puts some pressure on our center, but in reality, only makes their knight a target. We march forward and attack Black's knight with e5!} 10.e5 10...dxe5 {Black captures on e5.} 11.fxe5 {We recapture on e5. Black's knight is now under attack and, no matter where it moves, we'll continue with the crushing e6. White has a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e5 {Black attempts to strike at our center with ...e5, but this is simply a mistake. It's played 3% of the time. We continue with d5 and target Black's knight.} 7.d5 7...Na5 {Best and rarest is ...Na5. We continue by cramping Black's position with d6. We are ready to play Be3 if needed to cover the b6-square against Black's queen.} 8.d6 8...Nf6 {Black continues developing with ...Nf6.} 9.Nf3 {We continue with Nf3 and attack the e5-pawn, with an advantage. The only move that doesn't lose is ...b5. However, much more natural is ...Nc6, which can be met with Na3. Our e-pawn cannot be captured since ...Nxe4 can always be met with Qd5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black plays ...d6 12% of the time, controlling the e5-square and activating the c8-bishop. Black didn't do anything to prevent us from playing d5, so we should go for it and quickly begin an initiative. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black opts to jump the knight into e5. This is most common, but the knight on e5 is a target. We continue the initative by expanding in the center and attacking Black's knight with f4!} 8.f4 8...Ng6 {Black chooses to retreat the knight to g6. We continue with the simple and strong Nf3. After some aggressive pushes, it's time to develop logically.} 9.Nf3 9...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and attacks the e-pawn. This, however, proves to be a fatal mistake. We keep going forward with e5!} 10.e5 10...dxe5 {Black captures on e5.} 11.fxe5 {We recapture on e5 with the f-pawn. Black's position is awful and White is completely winning, with ideas of e6, Qa4+ and Bb5+. To rub the salt into Black's wounds, the d-pawn is immune to capture since ...Nxd5 can be met with Bb5+ and Black loses a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black plays ...d6 12% of the time, controlling the e5-square and activating the c8-bishop. Black didn't do anything to prevent us from playing d5, so we should go for it and quickly begin an initiative. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black opts to jump the knight into e5. This is most common, but the knight on e5 is a target. We continue the initative by expanding in the center and attacking Black's knight with f4!} 8.f4 8...Nd7 {Black opts to retreat the knight to d7. We continue by developing our knight to f3. This move also supports a central break with e5.} 9.Nf3 9...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto their dark-squared bishop with ...g6. While this is the most common continuation, it is quite inaccurate. We respond with Qd4! This move exposes the weakened dark squares and forks Black's rook and the a7-pawn.} 10.Qd4 10...f6 {Black might also defend the rook with ...f6.} 11.Bb5 {We'll continue with Bb5. This move actually prepares the very powerful Ng5-Ne6, since Black can no longer defend with ...Nc5. We are also threatening to win our pawn back by capturing on a7. White has a winning advantage and a very pleasant position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e5 {Black attempts to strike at our center with ...e5, but this is simply a mistake. It's played 3% of the time. We continue with d5 and target Black's knight.} 7.d5 7...Nce7 {Most common is ...Nce7, but this loses on the spot.} 8.d6 {We continue by attacking Black's knight with d6. After Black moves the attacked knight (to c6 or g6), we will continue with Na3 and there is no way for Black to prevent Nb5 and Nc7+. White is already completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...e5 {Black attempts to strike at our center with ...e5, but this is simply a mistake. It's played 3% of the time. We continue with d5 and target Black's knight.} 7.d5 7...Nb8 {Second most common is ...Nb8, but this also lands Black into a lost position. We continue with d6. This move cramps Black's position and prepares a future Nc7+.} 8.d6 8...Qb6 {Black tries to solve their problems by attacking the d-pawn with ...Qb6. We don't need to defend the pawn with a move like Ba3! Instead, we "ignore" the attack and continue with Na3!} 9.Na3 9...Qxd6 {Black captures the d-pawn. We can actually exchange queens on d6.} 10.Qxd6 10...Bxd6 {Black recaptures on d6. We continue with Nb5! This attacks Black's bishop, but also threatens Nc7+ if the bishop were to move.} 11.Nb5 11...Ke7 {Black's only try is to defend the bishop with the king. We'll capture the bishop on d6.} 12.Nxd6 12...Kxd6 {Black recaptures on d6. You know things have gone horribly wrong when Black's only developed piece is the king on the sixth rank. We'll continue with Ba3+, threatening Bf8 if Black's king moves to the c-file.} 13.Ba3+ 13...Ke6 {Black moves the king to e6.} 14.O-O-O {We continue by castling queenside. White has a winning advantage and Black's position makes no sense. We have various attacking ideas including Bc4, f4 and Nf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black plays ...d6 12% of the time, controlling the e5-square and activating the c8-bishop. Black didn't do anything to prevent us from playing d5, so we should go for it and quickly begin an initiative. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d5 7...Ne5 {Black opts to jump the knight into e5. This is most common, but the knight on e5 is a target. We continue the initative by expanding in the center and attacking Black's knight with f4!} 8.f4 8...Ng6 {Black chooses to retreat the knight to g6. We continue with the simple and strong Nf3. After some aggressive pushes, it's time to develop logically.} 9.Nf3 9...e6 {Black opens up the f8-bishop and tries to chip away at our center with ...e6. We continue by capturing the e-pawn. Black now cannot recapture with the bishop, since f5 would fork their bishop and knight.} 10.dxe6 10...fxe6 {Black recaptures with the f-pawn.} 11.Nd4 {We continue with Nd4. This move centralizes the knight and puts some pressure on the e6-pawn. White is better and has a very pleasant position. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4 or b5, our queen will generally develop to b3 and we will castle our king. A common tactical motif in these positions is Bb5+ Bd7 Nxe6!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: a3 Sicilian (Accepted) / Study 4: Black Plays 6...d6 and 6...e5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/66711176-2126-42e8-9689-551e2604ce3e/studies/0814bd44-3178-49f3-a68e-3c92b0dbe8b6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We already know that a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nxb4 {Black takes with the knight. We attack Black's knight with c3 and force it to retreat.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black retreats the knight back to c6. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black plays ...d6 12% of the time, controlling the e5-square and activating the c8-bishop. Black didn't do anything to prevent us from playing d5, so we should go for it and quickly begin an initiative. Black's knight is under attack.} 7.d5 7...Nb8 {Black opts to retreat the knight to b8, which is rarer, but objectively better. We develop with the nuanced Bb5+. This move ties Black's pieces down, as they have to block with either the bishop or the knight.} 8.Bb5+ 8...Bd7 {Black blocks the check and offers a trade with ...Bd7. We decline the trade and retreat the bishop to d3. Now the bishop is awkward on d7 and the knight has no squares to develop to.} 9.Bd3 9...g6 {Black continues with ...g6, preparing to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 10.Be3 {We continue by developing our bishop to e3 and putting some pressure on the a7-pawn. The position is objectively equal, but we have good compensation for the sacrificed pawn. We will continue to develop with Nf3, Qb3, Nd2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Half-Accepted (4...e5 and 4...d5) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e40ba1b4-9477-4330-9132-211710a4def8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...d5 {Black opts to decline the gambit by striking in the center with ...d5 only 2% of the time. This, however, really isn't a great move and will allow us to obtain a significant advantage. We march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 5.b5 5...Nd4 {Black opts to jump the knight into d4. Similarly to ...Ne5, on d4, the knight quickly becomes a target for our pawns. We attack Black's knight once again with c3.} 6.c3 6...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. We continue by capturing the d-pawn, forcing Black's queen to recapture.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5.} 8.d4 {We continue with d4. White has a very comfortable advantage: We have more space on the queenside and in the center. Black's e6-knight is clumsy and the queen on d5 might become a target. We will continue to develop with Nf3, Be2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Half-Accepted (4...e5 and 4...d5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e40ba1b4-9477-4330-9132-211710a4def8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...e5 {Black opts to decline the gambit by playing ...e5 just under 5% of the time. This is playable, but will allow us to quickly gain an initiative. We march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 5.b5 5...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We attack the knight once more with c3, forcing it to move again. Winning tempi like this in the opening is important.} 6.c3 6...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. We develop our knight to f3 and attack the e-pawn. Still attacking and winning tempi.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Black defends the e-pawn. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to c4. Our general development plan consists of d3, O-O, Be3 and h3.} 8.Bc4 8...Nf6 {Black chooses to attack our e4-pawn now, developing with tempo themselves. We respond with d3. This move supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 9.d3 9...Be7 {Black continues naturally developing with ...Be7. We continue with our plan and castle our king.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black also castles their king.} 11.Be3 {We continue with Be3, developing a piece and targeting the weak a-pawn. White has a comfortable advantage and we will continue to improve our position with moves like h3, Qb3 and Nbd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Half-Accepted (4...e5 and 4...d5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e40ba1b4-9477-4330-9132-211710a4def8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...e5 {Black opts to decline the gambit by playing ...e5 just under 5% of the time. This is playable, but will allow us to quickly gain an initiative. We march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 5.b5 5...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We attack the knight once more with c3, forcing it to move again. Winning tempi like this in the opening is important.} 6.c3 6...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. We develop our knight to f3 and attack the e-pawn. Still attacking and winning tempi.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black can also play the critical ...Nf6, counterattacking the e4- pawn! We'll take on e5 and let them capture on e4. The opening of the e-file will benefit us in the long run.} 8.Nxe5 8...Nxe4 {Black captures the e4-pawn. We'll proceed with d4, expanding in the center and supporting our knight.} 9.d4 9...d6 {Black attacks our knight and forces it to move. We retreat the knight back to f3. The most natural square.} 10.Nf3 10...d5 {...d5 increases Black's central presence and supports the knight.} 11.Bd3 {We continue by developing our bishop to d3. This move puts some pressure on Black's strong knight and prepares O-O. White has a slight advantage. We will castle on the next move and can try to remove Black's outposted knight with moves like c4, Re1 and/or Nbd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Half-Accepted (4...e5 and 4...d5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e40ba1b4-9477-4330-9132-211710a4def8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...d5 {Black opts to decline the gambit by striking in the center with ...d5 only 2% of the time. This, however, really isn't a great move and will allow us to obtain a significant advantage. We march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 5.b5 5...Ne5 {Most often, Black jumps the knight into e5. On e5, the knight quickly becomes a target for our pawns. We attack Black's knight once again with d4!} 6.d4 6...Ng6 {Black moves the knight to g6. We continue by marching forward and gaining even more space with e5. Once we have a pawn on d4, pushing and claiming more territory is superior to taking on d5.} 7.e5 7...Bf5 {Black develops the bishop to f5. Similarly to the advance variation of the Caro-Kann, Black wants to follow up with ...e6 and have their bishop outside of the pawn chain. We lash out with g4! This forces Black's bishop to retreat.} 8.g4 8...Bd7 {Black retreats the bishop to d7. We follow our previous move up with h4, gaining even more space and threatening to trap Black's knight with h5! Even though we will have a pretty extended structure, it's worth pushing here to suffocate Black's pieces.} 9.h4 9...e6 {Black continues with ...e6, solidifying the structure and freeing up the e7-square for their knight. We continue marching forward with h5!} 10.h5 10...N6e7 {Black retreats their knight to e7.} 11.Nf3 {We simply develop with Nf3. White is already completely winning and Black is literally gasping for air. We have many ways to continue improving our position, but some general ideas include Bd3, Bd2 and c3. Notable is that we don't need to rush with castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Half-Accepted (4...e5 and 4...d5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e40ba1b4-9477-4330-9132-211710a4def8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...d5 {Black opts to decline the gambit by striking in the center with ...d5 only 2% of the time. This, however, really isn't a great move and will allow us to obtain a significant advantage. We march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 5.b5 5...Ne5 {Most often, Black jumps the knight into e5. On e5, the knight quickly becomes a target for our pawns. We attack Black's knight once again with d4!} 6.d4 6...Ng6 {Black moves the knight to g6. We continue by marching forward and gaining even more space with e5. Once we have a pawn on d4, pushing and claiming more territory is superior to taking on d5.} 7.e5 7...e6 {Black can also play ...e6 immediately, entering a French Defense without a ...c5-break and with a misplaced knight on g6.} 8.Bd3 {We develop our bishop to d3. According to the engine, this position is borderline winning for White. We will continue in "typical French style" with c3 and Nf3. One of our main ideas is also h4, followed by h5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Half-Accepted (4...e5 and 4...d5) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e40ba1b4-9477-4330-9132-211710a4def8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...d5 {Black opts to decline the gambit by striking in the center with ...d5 only 2% of the time. This, however, really isn't a great move and will allow us to obtain a significant advantage. We march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 5.b5 5...Nb8 {Black can also retreat the knight to b8, out of any immediate danger. We continue by capturing the d-pawn. Black might try to delay recapturing with ...Nf6, but a simple and strong way to meet that idea is c4.} 6.exd5 6...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5.} 7.Nf3 {We shouldn't rush to attack Black's queen with Nc3, and should instead simply develop with Nf3. Black can continue in a variety of ways, but our general plan consists of Be2, O-O, d4 and c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e5 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...e5, and they do so 4% of the time. This improves the dark-squared presence in the center and grabs some space, but it leaves the d5-square weak. We'll march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We attack the knight again with c3, naturally.} 5.c3 5...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to c4. This move also claims that d5-square.} 6.Bc4 6...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn. We simply defend and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...Be7 {Instead of striking in the center with ...d5, Black continues naturally developing with ...Be7. We develop our knight to f3 and attack the e-pawn now.} 8.Nf3 8...d6 {Black defends. Let's castle out of the way.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black also castles their king.} 10.a4 {We play a4 in order to support the b-pawn and free up the a3-square. White has a pleasant position and a modest advantage. We will continue to improve our position with moves like Qb3, Na3, Re1 and Be3. Keeping control of those light squares is important.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e6 {Black doesn't capture on b4 and declines the gambit with ...e6 around 3% of the time. This is more solid but also a more passive option for Black. They might want to prepare ...d5. We will play b5 anyway and attack the knight.} 4.b5 4...Ne5 {Most natural and most common is ...Ne5, centralizing the knight. We continue by developing our dark-squared bishop and attacking Black's knight. This activates our bishop on the long diagonal with tempo.} 5.Bb2 5...d6 {Black defends the knight with ...d6. We continue with a4, supporting the b5-pawn and freeing up the a3-square for our knight if we need it. Either way, it's a useful positional move.} 6.a4 6...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and attacks our e-pawn. We'll simply defend.} 7.d3 7...Be7 {Black develops with ...Be7.} 8.Nd2 {We develop with Nd2. White has a slight edge in this position. We will develop our bishop to e2 and aim to expand on the kingside with f4, g3 and h4. We might also prepare to castle kingside by developing our knight to f3 or h3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e5 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...e5, and they do so 4% of the time. This improves the dark-squared presence in the center and grabs some space, but it leaves the d5-square weak. We'll march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We attack the knight again with c3, naturally.} 5.c3 5...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to c4. This move also claims that d5-square.} 6.Bc4 6...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn. We simply defend and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...d5 {Black strikes in the center with ...d5. An ambitious move where Black extends all three pawns in the center in hopes of maximum aggression. We'll simply capture on d5 for now.} 8.exd5 8...Nxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We develop our knight to f3 and attack the e-pawn, which is not defended.} 9.Nf3 9...f6 {Black defends the e-pawn with ...f6, but this is inaccurate.} 10.O-O {We continue with our plan and castle our king. White has a comfortable advantage and the threat is now Nxe5, followed by Qh5+. If Black ever attacks our bishop with ...Nb6, we can safely retreat it to a2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e5 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...e5, and they do so 4% of the time. This improves the dark-squared presence in the center and grabs some space, but it leaves the d5-square weak. We'll march forward and attack the knight with b5!} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We attack the knight again with c3, naturally.} 5.c3 5...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to c4. This move also claims that d5-square.} 6.Bc4 6...Nf6 {Black develops with ...Nf6 and attacks our e-pawn. We simply defend and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...d5 {Black strikes in the center with ...d5. An ambitious move where Black extends all three pawns in the center in hopes of maximum aggression. We'll simply capture on d5 for now.} 8.exd5 8...Nxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We develop our knight to f3 and attack the e-pawn, which is not defended.} 9.Nf3 9...Nb6 {Black plays ...Nb6 and attacks our bishop. This is the critical move.} 10.Ba2 {We retreat the bishop to a2. The position is equal, but Black needs to play accurately. We will castle kingside and continue to improve our position with a4, Nbd2 and Re1. Even though it seems like Black has the space advantage in the center, their pawns don't have a lot of prospects.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e6 {Black doesn't capture on b4 and declines the gambit with ...e6 around 3% of the time. This is more solid but also a more passive option for Black. They might want to prepare ...d5. We will play b5 anyway and attack the knight.} 4.b5 4...Ne5 {Most natural and most common is ...Ne5, centralizing the knight. We continue by developing our dark-squared bishop and attacking Black's knight. This activates our bishop on the long diagonal with tempo.} 5.Bb2 5...Ng6 {Black retreats the knight to g6. In this case, we'll just continue developing.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {Black goes for the e4-pawn. Let's push it and counterattack.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5.} 8.c4 {We continue by attacking Black's knight with c4. With a lead in development and a space advantage, White has a very pleasant position. We will continue standard development with g3, Bg2 and castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e6 {Black doesn't capture on b4 and declines the gambit with ...e6 around 3% of the time. This is more solid but also a more passive option for Black. They might want to prepare ...d5. We will play b5 anyway and attack the knight.} 4.b5 4...Ne5 {Most natural and most common is ...Ne5, centralizing the knight. We continue by developing our dark-squared bishop and attacking Black's knight. This activates our bishop on the long diagonal with tempo.} 5.Bb2 5...Ng6 {Black retreats the knight to g6. In this case, we'll just continue developing.} 6.Nf3 6...d5 {Black correctly strikes in the center with ...d5. Challenging our center and trying to restrict our dark-squared bishop is critical. That's why we'll capture on d5.} 7.exd5 7...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn.} 8.Qe2+ {The position is objectively equal, but easier to play for White. We will simply develop with g3, Bg2 and O-O, and Black still has to figure out how to safeguard their king and develop the f8-bishop. If Black blocks with the queen here, we should trade queens and continue with our plan.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e6 {Black doesn't capture on b4 and declines the gambit with ...e6 around 3% of the time. This is more solid but also a more passive option for Black. They might want to prepare ...d5. We will play b5 anyway and attack the knight.} 4.b5 4...Nb8 {Black opts to retreat the knight to b8. The ideas here are pretty much the same as those in the variations with 4...Ne5. We continue by developing our dark-squared bishop to b2.} 5.Bb2 5...d5 {Black strikes in the center with ...d5. It's best to capture the d-pawn.} 6.exd5 6...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn.} 7.Qe2+ {We continue with Qe2+. The position is fairly equal. We will continue to develop with Nf3, g3, Bg2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e6 {Black doesn't capture on b4 and declines the gambit with ...e6 around 3% of the time. This is more solid but also a more passive option for Black. They might want to prepare ...d5. We will play b5 anyway and attack the knight.} 4.b5 4...Nb8 {Black opts to retreat the knight to b8. The ideas here are pretty much the same as those in the variations with 4...Ne5. We continue by developing our dark-squared bishop to b2.} 5.Bb2 5...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 and attacks the e-pawn. We push forward and attack Black's knight with e5! Sometimes aggression is the best defense.} 6.e5 6...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5.} 7.c4 {We continue by attacking Black's knight with c4. With a lead in development and a space advantage, White has a very pleasant position. We will continue developing with Nf3, g3, Bg2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e6 {Black doesn't capture on b4 and declines the gambit with ...e6 around 3% of the time. This is more solid but also a more passive option for Black. They might want to prepare ...d5. We will play b5 anyway and attack the knight.} 4.b5 4...Nce7 {Black can also retreat the knight to e7, now definitely aiming for ...d5. We continue with g3, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 5.g3 5...d5 {Black strikes in the center with ...d5. We'll capture the d-pawn.} 6.exd5 6...exd5 {Black recaptures with the d-pawn.} 7.Bg2 {We continue with Bg2. This is alike the variations with 4...Ne5 and 4...Nb8. The position is objectively equal and we will continue to develop with Nf3, Bb2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 2: Black Plays 3...e6 and 3...e5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/e7e77607-8190-4bec-91ca-5fabf52f4a3e/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. a3 prepares b4.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We respond by offering up a pawn with b4!} 3.b4 3...e6 {Black doesn't capture on b4 and declines the gambit with ...e6 around 3% of the time. This is more solid but also a more passive option for Black. They might want to prepare ...d5. We will play b5 anyway and attack the knight.} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {This is actually a very common blunder.} 5.c3 {Just go c3. The knight is out of squares!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...d6 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...d6. This happens 2% of the time. We'll attack the knight with b5 once again.} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {Black opts to jump the knight into d4. We'll attack the knight with c3, as per usual.} 5.c3 5...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. This gives us time to strike in the center with d4.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black captures on d4. We recapture with the c-pawn, naturally.} 7.cxd4 7...g6 {Black can also play ...g6 before developing their knight to f6. This is the critical approach. Black wants to fianchetto and fight on the diagonal that we've opened.} 8.Nf3 8...Bg7 {Black develops their bishop to g7, putting more pressure on the d-pawn. We can develop our other knight to c3 for now. Let's take a look at the most challenging response from Black.} 9.Nc3 9...Qa5 {Black moves their queen out to a5 and attacks our c3-knight. If we move it, we get in the way of our own pieces and relinquish some central control. So we'll defend it instead, leaving our d4-pawn hanging.} 10.Bd2 10...Nxd4 {Black captures the d-pawn. We'll trade once.} 11.Nxd4 11...Bxd4 {Black recaptures on d4. But here's the trick. We jump our knight into d5, opening up an attack on Black's queen and threatening Nc7+!} 12.Nd5 12...Qd8 {Black retreats the queen to d8, moving the queen out of the attack and defending against the fork, which was the only sensible move. Our rook was attacked, so we'll move it to c1 from where it supports the c7-square. An active couple of moves.} 13.Rc1 13...Bb6 {Black retreats their bishop to b6 in order to defend against Nc7+, which was the threat once again.} 14.Bc4 {We continue by developing our bishop to c4. The position is objectively equal. We are down a pawn, but Black is underdeveloped and we have sufficient dynamic compensation as well as activity. We'll continue by castling our king and play will develop from there.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...Nf6 {Black develops the knight to f6 and attacks the e-pawn 1% of the time. Interestingly enough, 2...Nc6 combined with 3...Nf6 is one of the most critical ways to fight the a3 Sicilian. We begin an initiative by attacking Black's knight with b5, though.} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We attack Black's knight again with c3. Motifs we've seen before.} 5.c3 5...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. But we won't let the aggression down. We play e5 and attack Black's other knight, grabbing a lot of valuable space while pushing Black's pieces backwards.} 6.e5 6...Nd5 {Black jumps the attacked knight into d5. Let's keep attacking! We play Qb3 and attack Black's knight one more time, still developing and expanding with tempo.} 7.Qb3 7...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight to b6. The initiative doesn't stop! We continue with a4, threatening to trap Black's knight with a5.} 8.a4 8...d6 {Black fights back with ...d6. This move attacks our e-pawn and frees up the e7-square for the knight on b6. However, the best option is to disregard Black's counterplay attempt, and attack Black's knight with a5!} 9.a5 9...Nd7 {Black retreats the knight to the only safe square.} 10.Nf3 {We ignore the threat on our e-pawn again and continue developing with Nf3. The position is unclear. Black can continue by capturing the e-pawn, but the critical move is actually ...g5. To better your understanding of this position, I suggest you take a look at the game Belka, Wieland - Domancich, Ezequiel Martín (2018).} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...d6 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...d6. This happens 2% of the time. We'll attack the knight with b5 once again.} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {Black opts to jump the knight into d4. We'll attack the knight with c3, as per usual.} 5.c3 5...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. This gives us time to strike in the center with d4.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black captures on d4. We recapture with the c-pawn, naturally.} 7.cxd4 7...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 and attacks our e-pawn. We have to support the pawn with f3. Any other move would be too dangerous.} 8.f3 8...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto.} 9.Be3 {We continue by developing our bishop to e3. With more space in the center and on the queenside, White has a pleasant position and an objective edge. We'll continue developing with Bd3, Ne2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...d6 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...d6. This happens 2% of the time. We'll attack the knight with b5 once again.} 4.b5 4...Ne5 {Most often, Black jumps the knight into e5. Let's offer a knight trade to try and remove Black's active knight from the center.} 5.Nf3 5...Nxf3+ {Black captures on f3. We recapture with the queen as to keep our kingside pawn structure intact.} 6.Qxf3 6...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6.} 7.Bb2 {We continue by developing our bishop to b2 on its most active diagonal. The position is, again, objectively equal. We'll continue to develop with Be2, O-O and a4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...d6 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...d6. This happens 2% of the time. We'll attack the knight with b5 once again.} 4.b5 4...Nd4 {Black opts to jump the knight into d4. We'll attack the knight with c3, as per usual.} 5.c3 5...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6. This gives us time to strike in the center with d4.} 6.d4 6...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 and attacks our e-pawn. In this line, we already have two pawns on e4 and d4. So we'll respond with d5, counterattacking Black's knight!} 7.d5 7...Nc7 {Black retreats the knight to c7.} 8.Nd2 {We continue with Nd2, defending the e-pawn. The position is close to equality, but White certainly has a slight edge due to the space advantage. We'll continue to develop and improve our position with Ngf3, Be2, Bb2, c4 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...b6 {Black declines the gambit with ...b6 extremely rarely, preparing to develop to b7. We continue with the simple and effective Nf3. This move develops a piece and supports a future d4. If Black ever captures on b4, we'll be able to put another pawn in the center with d4.} 4.Nf3 4...Bb7 {Black continues with their plan and develops the bishop to b7. We play c3. The main purpose of this move is to support a future central expansion with d4. Black now has to continue accurately as there is only one move which keeps the equality.} 5.c3 5...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 and attacks the e-pawn. This is the critical move. If Black hadn't played ...Nf6, we would've simply been able to respond with d4. But, we march forward and attack the knight with e5 instead!} 6.e5 6...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We continue the initiative by developing our bishop to c4 and attacking Black's knight. These tempo moves in the opening might get you a long way.} 7.Bc4 7...e6 {Black defends the knight with ...e6. Let's just castle for now. We don't have to rush.} 8.O-O 8...d6 {Black tries to undermine our e-pawn. Let's expand in the center and support it.} 9.d4 9...dxe5 {Black captures on e5.} 10.dxe5 {We'll recapture. The position is complicated but objectively equal. We'll continue to develop with Qe2 and Rd1/Re1. A typical attacking idea is to attack Black's castled king with Bd3, Qe2 and Qe4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...d6 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...d6. This happens 2% of the time. We'll attack the knight with b5 once again.} 4.b5 4...Ne5 {Most often, Black jumps the knight into e5. Let's offer a knight trade to try and remove Black's active knight from the center.} 5.Nf3 5...Bg4 {Black develops their bishop and pins our knight.} 6.Be2 {We develop with Be2, unpinning the knight. The position is objectively equal. We'll continue to develop with O-O, Nc3, Bb2 and a4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: a3 Sicilian (Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6, 3...d6, 3...Nf6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/15cf659a-40cc-4748-b1ec-958ad64de148/studies/70bcaebf-cf95-49bb-ab9d-b7b70f91f7f4/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. The move a3 prepares our gambit.} 2.a3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll offer a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...d6 {Black can also decline the gambit with ...d6. This happens 2% of the time. We'll attack the knight with b5 once again.} 4.b5 4...Nb8 {Black can also retreat the attacked knight to b8. We'll continue with c4, supporting the b5-pawn and preventing any future ...d5 ideas. If Black ever tries to undermine our structure with ...a6, we can respond with a4.} 5.c4 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll defend.} 6.d3 6...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto their dark-squared bishop now. However, we'll be the first ones to occupy the long diagonal. With the dark squares open, it makes the most sense to place the bishop on b2.} 7.Bb2 7...Bg7 {Black develops their bishop to g7, preparing ...O-O.} 8.a4 {We continue with a4. The position is objectively equal, but marginally more pleasant for White. We will continue to develop with g3, Bg2, Nf3, Nc3 and O-O. Simply activating the pieces to their natural squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 1: Black Plays 2...g6 (e6 & Nge7) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/4494aefb-840e-40c8-9419-4d20d2c8bf57/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for a3.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players, and it's played 7% of the time here. Black is preparing ...Bg7. It's not the best for White to continue with b4, as the weakening of the a1-h8 diagonal only plays into Black's hands. Instead, I recommend the setup: Nc3, Bc4, d3, preparing h4.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll play d3, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop and supporting the e-pawn.} 5.d3 5...e6 {Black plays ...e6 in order to develop their knight to e7 and prepare a central expansion with ...d5. It's the most common move, played almost 40% of the time. It's important to invest a full move retreating the bishop to a2, since we don't want it to get hit with the eventual ...d5.} 6.Ba2 6...Nge7 {Black develops their knight to e7, preparing O-O and supporting ...d5. We continue with h4, as we want to play h5 and attack Black's kingside. If Black now castles their king, the attack with h5 is very promising for White.} 7.h4 7...h5 {Most common is ...h5. This move physically prevents h5 directly, but also weakens the dark-squares on the kingside. We immediately make use of this by developing our bishop to g5. Black can't play ...h6 to kick the bishop away.} 8.Bg5 8...d6 {Black continues naturally.} 9.Qd2 {We continue with Qd2, with the idea to "connect the rooks" and support a future Bh6. The computer gives equality, but the position is very rich and double-edged. Black's counterplay is centered around the queenside attack with ...a6, ...Rb8 and ...b5, so we will look to develop with Ne2/Nf3 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 1: Black Plays 2...g6 (e6 & Nge7) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/4494aefb-840e-40c8-9419-4d20d2c8bf57/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for a3.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players, and it's played 7% of the time here. Black is preparing ...Bg7. It's not the best for White to continue with b4, as the weakening of the a1-h8 diagonal only plays into Black's hands. Instead, I recommend the setup: Nc3, Bc4, d3, preparing h4.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll play d3, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop and supporting the e-pawn.} 5.d3 5...e6 {Black plays ...e6 in order to develop their knight to e7 and prepare a central expansion with ...d5. It's the most common move, played almost 40% of the time. It's important to invest a full move retreating the bishop to a2, since we don't want it to get hit with the eventual ...d5.} 6.Ba2 6...Nge7 {Black develops their knight to e7, preparing O-O and supporting ...d5. We continue with h4, as we want to play h5 and attack Black's kingside. If Black now castles their king, the attack with h5 is very promising for White.} 7.h4 7...h6 {Black might also go for ...h6, but this turns out to be slightly inaccurate. We continue with h5, looking to open the kingside!} 8.h5 8...g5 {Black tries to keep the kingside closed with ...g5, which was their whole idea. But we can continue poking. The f-pawn is looking to do some damage to Black's structure now.} 9.f4 9...gxf4 {Black captures on f4.} 10.Bxf4 {We recapture with the bishop. White has a slight advantage and we will continue to develop with Nf3 and Qd2. Our king can castle queenside, but in many cases, especially if Black chooses to play with ...d5, it is even better for us to keep it in the center of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/a15ae36c-a663-4667-ad20-912c4c4fb761/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play a3.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3 as recommended.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll develop the bishop next.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll support the light squares in the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black plays ...d6 now, opening up the light-squared bishop. This move is third in popularity and it's played 20% of the time. Against 5...d6, we will also continue in "Vienna" style with f4, Nf3 and O-O.} 6.f4 6...e6 {20% of the time, Black plays ...e6 in order to develop their knight to e7 and prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...Nge7 {Black develops their knight to e7, preparing ...O-O and supporting the potential central thrust. The idea of Ba2 keeps repeating itself!} 8.Ba2 8...O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. Surprisingly enough, this is an inaccuracy and allows us to kickstart an attack! We strike with h4, aiming to play h5 and attack Black's kingside.} 9.h4 9...h5 {Most common is ...h5. We already know the downsides of this move, though. That's why we immediately jump into g5.} 10.Ng5 10...d5 {Black tries to fight back in the center with ...d5.} 11.g4 {But we are quicker on the kingside with g4, with a raging kingside attack and an advantage. If Black kicks our knight out with ...f6, we should retreat it to h3. Otherwise, we can consider capturing on h5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/a15ae36c-a663-4667-ad20-912c4c4fb761/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play a3.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3 as recommended.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll develop the bishop next.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll support the light squares in the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black plays ...d6 now, opening up the light-squared bishop. This move is third in popularity and it's played 20% of the time. Against 5...d6, we will also continue in "Vienna" style with f4, Nf3 and O-O.} 6.f4 6...e6 {20% of the time, Black plays ...e6 in order to develop their knight to e7 and prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...Nge7 {Black develops their knight to e7, preparing ...O-O and supporting the potential central thrust. The idea of Ba2 keeps repeating itself!} 8.Ba2 8...O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. Surprisingly enough, this is an inaccuracy and allows us to kickstart an attack! We strike with h4, aiming to play h5 and attack Black's kingside.} 9.h4 9...h5 {Most common is ...h5. We already know the downsides of this move, though. That's why we immediately jump into g5.} 10.Ng5 10...Nd4 {Black jumps to d4. The main idea behind this move is to prepare ...f6 by defending the e6-pawn.} 11.Ne2 {We move our knight to e2 in order to trade off the d4-knight and attack Black's kingside with g4. White has a pleasant advantage, a promising attack and great winning chances.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/a15ae36c-a663-4667-ad20-912c4c4fb761/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play a3.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3 as recommended.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll develop the bishop next.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll support the light squares in the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black plays ...d6 now, opening up the light-squared bishop. This move is third in popularity and it's played 20% of the time. Against 5...d6, we will also continue in "Vienna" style with f4, Nf3 and O-O.} 6.f4 6...e6 {20% of the time, Black plays ...e6 in order to develop their knight to e7 and prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...Nge7 {Black develops their knight to e7, preparing ...O-O and supporting the potential central thrust. The idea of Ba2 keeps repeating itself!} 8.Ba2 8...O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. Surprisingly enough, this is an inaccuracy and allows us to kickstart an attack! We strike with h4, aiming to play h5 and attack Black's kingside.} 9.h4 9...d5 {The best way for Black to fight against h4 and the oncoming attack is to immediately play in the center with ...d5 and ...Nd4. We continue the attack with h5. We want to capture on g6 and open the h-file. If Black captures on e4, we should recapture with the knight as to keep the queens on the board.} 10.h5 10...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We shouldn't rush to capture on g6! A better option is Ne2, intending to trade off Black's strong knight on d4. If Black now captures on e4, we should capture on d4 with our f-knight.} 11.Ne2 11...Nxf3+ {Black captures on f3.} 12.gxf3 {We recapture on f3. White has a slight advantage and great attacking chances with the fascinating Kf2 and Qg1. The idea is to make use of the semi-open g-file and bring as many pieces to the attack as possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/a15ae36c-a663-4667-ad20-912c4c4fb761/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play a3.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3 as recommended.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll develop the bishop next.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll support the light squares in the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black plays ...d6 now, opening up the light-squared bishop. This move is third in popularity and it's played 20% of the time. Against 5...d6, we will also continue in "Vienna" style with f4, Nf3 and O-O.} 6.f4 6...e6 {20% of the time, Black plays ...e6 in order to develop their knight to e7 and prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...Nge7 {Black develops their knight to e7, preparing ...O-O and supporting the potential central thrust. The idea of Ba2 keeps repeating itself!} 8.Ba2 8...d5 {Black immediately strikes in the center with ...d5, which is the critical choice. We'll continue by castling kingside. If Black now captures on e4, we should recapture with the d-pawn.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black also castles their king.} 10.e5 {We close the center and the diagonal with e5, preparing our plan with Ne2 and Qe1. Notable is that after Ne2 and Qe1, any Black knight on f5 can be kicked out with g4. If Black quickly tries to "break out" with ...f6, we should capture and continue with our plan.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/a15ae36c-a663-4667-ad20-912c4c4fb761/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play a3.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3 as recommended.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll develop the bishop next.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll support the light squares in the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black plays ...d6 now, opening up the light-squared bishop. This move is third in popularity and it's played 20% of the time. Against 5...d6, we will also continue in "Vienna" style with f4, Nf3 and O-O.} 6.f4 6...Nf6 {Nf6 is actually the most common move, but this is simply a transposition to the next study. It's played 58% of the time. Refer to Study 3, variation 6...d6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 3: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/14603925-06e4-481c-8133-c043ad7ca918/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play a3 to try b4.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll connect the pawns.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight to f6 almost 30% of the time. This is slightly less common than 5...e6 and yields different positions. Against ...Nf6, we'll give up on the idea of h4, and instead continue in "Vienna" style.} 6.f4 6...O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. And we continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Black can also play ...d6, supporting the c-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We should play h3 to take control of the g4-square.} 8.h3 8...a6 {In typical Sicilian fashion, Black is preparing to expand on the queenside with ...b5. There is no need to be afraid of the queenside expansion. We can simply continue by castling kingside.} 9.O-O 9...b5 {Black continues with their plan by expanding with ...b5 and attacking our c4-bishop.} 10.Ba2 {The bishop was attacked, so we retreat it to a2. Play can develop in a variety of ways, but some typical attacking ideas include Qe1-Qh4, Ne2 and a timely f5. Be aware of Black pushing ...b4 and the potential ...Qb6+ after the trades on b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 3: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/14603925-06e4-481c-8133-c043ad7ca918/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play a3 to try b4.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll connect the pawns.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight to f6 almost 30% of the time. This is slightly less common than 5...e6 and yields different positions. Against ...Nf6, we'll give up on the idea of h4, and instead continue in "Vienna" style.} 6.f4 6...O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. And we continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...e6 {Slightly more common is ...e6. As we've seen in the previous study, Black plays ...e6 in order to prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We'll implement the Ba2 idea we learned in the previous studies.} 8.Ba2 8...d5 {Black follows up their previous move by expanding with ...d5.} 9.O-O {We castle our king. It is pretty hard to suggest some general systematic ideas, but we will aim to close the center with e5 and go for the already mentioned Nb5-Nd6 plan. The computer thinks this position is equal, but I like White's chances.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 3: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
