1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play a3 to try b4.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll connect the pawns.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight to f6 almost 30% of the time. This is slightly less common than 5...e6 and yields different positions. Against ...Nf6, we'll give up on the idea of h4, and instead continue in "Vienna" style.} 6.f4 6...O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. And we continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...e6 {Slightly more common is ...e6. As we've seen in the previous study, Black plays ...e6 in order to prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We'll implement the Ba2 idea we learned in the previous studies.} 8.Ba2 8...d6 {Black can also decide to be a bit more "solid" with ...d6, although this leads to a more passive setup.} 9.O-O {Similarly to the main continuation, the computer gives equality, but the position is still rich and complicated. We have interesting attacking ideas involving Qe1-Qh4 and f5. A move like ...d5 is simply a loss of time and we should respond by closing the center with e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 3: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...Nf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/14603925-06e4-481c-8133-c043ad7ca918/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play a3 to try b4.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll connect the pawns.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight to f6 almost 30% of the time. This is slightly less common than 5...e6 and yields different positions. Against ...Nf6, we'll give up on the idea of h4, and instead continue in "Vienna" style.} 6.f4 6...O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. And we continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Black can also play ...d6, supporting the c-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We should play h3 to take control of the g4-square.} 8.h3 8...e6 {The other option that Black might go for is ...e6, preparing to expand with ...d5. We castle our king in response and let them play ...d5 if they want to.} 9.O-O 9...d5 {Black follows up their previous move by expanding.} 10.Ba2 {Interestingly, the engine actually prefers the inclusion of h3 and gives a slight edge to White. Similarly to the other position, we will aim to close the center with e5 and can try to follow it up with Nb5-Nd6. If Black captures on e4, it is important that we recapture with the d-pawn!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black Plays The 2...g6 Fianchetto / Study 3: Black Plays 2...g6 and 5...Nf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/805ef1e9-5632-4a24-83b1-1846512fa0d6/studies/14603925-06e4-481c-8133-c043ad7ca918/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play a3 to try b4.} 2.a3 2...g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll connect the pawns.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight to f6 almost 30% of the time. This is slightly less common than 5...e6 and yields different positions. Against ...Nf6, we'll give up on the idea of h4, and instead continue in "Vienna" style.} 6.f4 6...d6 {Black can also continue with ...d6, but this quickly transposes. We'll continue with h3 in order to prevent Black's light-squared bishop from developing to g4.} 7.h3 7...O-O {Black castles their king.} 8.Nf3 {We develop our knight and we've transposed to the 7...d6 lines.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/9e1c4652-9584-446c-9414-f22bddc7b5e5/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black plays ...e6 on move two 17% of the time, and it happened in more than 600,000 games. By playing ...e6, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing ...d5. We will offer a pawn with b4, which is the main idea behind a3.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 60% of the time. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black decides to take on b4 again almost 90% of the time. We'll kick away Black's bishop with c3. The move c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {Black strikes back in the center with ...d5 most frequently, 40% of the time. We'll continue by closing the center with e5. We get a pawn structure that resembles the French, except Black doesn't have the ...c5 break.} 7.e5 7...Nc6 {Black continues developing with ...Nc6 around 58% of the time. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. On a side note, if Black ever plays ...Nh6, we will capture that knight.} 8.Bd3 8...f6 {Black might try to chip away at our structure with ...f6, but this is simply too weakening. We immediately expose Black's previous move by bringing our queen out to h5! Of course, if Black were to block with ...g6, we have the winning tactic with Bxg6.} 9.Qh5+ 9...Kf8 {Black slides the king over to f8.} 10.Nf3 {We simply develop our knight to f3, preparing O-O. With a dominant space advantage and better piece activity, White is already much better, and it is unclear how Black should even attempt to untangle. Any attempt to close the position with ...f5 can be met with g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/9e1c4652-9584-446c-9414-f22bddc7b5e5/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black plays ...e6 on move two 17% of the time, and it happened in more than 600,000 games. By playing ...e6, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing ...d5. We will offer a pawn with b4, which is the main idea behind a3.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 60% of the time. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black decides to take on b4 again almost 90% of the time. We'll kick away Black's bishop with c3. The move c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {Black strikes back in the center with ...d5 most frequently, 40% of the time. We'll continue by closing the center with e5. We get a pawn structure that resembles the French, except Black doesn't have the ...c5 break.} 7.e5 7...Nc6 {Black continues developing with ...Nc6 around 58% of the time. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. On a side note, if Black ever plays ...Nh6, we will capture that knight.} 8.Bd3 8...f5 {Another common move players go for is the immediate ...f5, looking to grab some central space and close the position.} 9.h4 {We continue with h4, preventing the annoying ...Bg5! We'll proceed with g4, and a common idea is to bring the g-knight to f4 via h3 or e2. Our h-pawn is currently untouchable because of Qh5+ threats. We are borderline winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/9e1c4652-9584-446c-9414-f22bddc7b5e5/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black plays ...e6 on move two 17% of the time, and it happened in more than 600,000 games. By playing ...e6, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing ...d5. We will offer a pawn with b4, which is the main idea behind a3.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 60% of the time. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black decides to take on b4 again almost 90% of the time. We'll kick away Black's bishop with c3. The move c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {Black strikes back in the center with ...d5 most frequently, 40% of the time. We'll continue by closing the center with e5. We get a pawn structure that resembles the French, except Black doesn't have the ...c5 break.} 7.e5 7...Nc6 {Black continues developing with ...Nc6 around 58% of the time. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. On a side note, if Black ever plays ...Nh6, we will capture that knight.} 8.Bd3 8...Bd7 {Black moves their bishop to d7. We quickly develop our queen and attack the vulnerable g7-pawn. This is one of the downsides of moving the dark-squared bishop early for Black.} 9.Qg4 9...g6 {Most often, Black defends the g-pawn with ...g6. But this prompts an aggressive idea. We continue with h4! We want to play h5 and attack Black's kingside.} 10.h4 10...h5 {Black prevents h5 by playing ...h5 and attacking our queen. We'll simply slide it back to g3.} 11.Qg3 11...Nh6 {Black continues developing with ...Nh6. The knight wants to control the light squares on the kingside.} 12.Ne2 {We are down a pawn, but have sufficient compensation with the space advantage and superior piece activity. A surprisingly common motif in these positions is the knight sacrifice on g6. If Black now chooses to jump their knight into f5, we can simply capture and proceed by bringing our knight to f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/9e1c4652-9584-446c-9414-f22bddc7b5e5/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black plays ...e6 on move two 17% of the time, and it happened in more than 600,000 games. By playing ...e6, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing ...d5. We will offer a pawn with b4, which is the main idea behind a3.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 60% of the time. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black decides to take on b4 again almost 90% of the time. We'll kick away Black's bishop with c3. The move c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {Black strikes back in the center with ...d5 most frequently, 40% of the time. We'll continue by closing the center with e5. We get a pawn structure that resembles the French, except Black doesn't have the ...c5 break.} 7.e5 7...Nc6 {Black continues developing with ...Nc6 around 58% of the time. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. On a side note, if Black ever plays ...Nh6, we will capture that knight.} 8.Bd3 8...Bd7 {Black moves their bishop to d7. We quickly develop our queen and attack the vulnerable g7-pawn. This is one of the downsides of moving the dark-squared bishop early for Black.} 9.Qg4 9...Kf8 {Black can also defend the g-pawn with the king. This might seem somewhat awkward at first glance, but we don't actually have a great way to expose the king's positioning.} 10.Na3 {We'll continue by developing our knight to a3. We want to control c4 and jump to c2 if needed rather than aim for b5. We'll continue to develop with Ne2 and O-O in this rich and complex position. Our dark-squared bishop can develop to e3, and our rook can go to b1.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/9e1c4652-9584-446c-9414-f22bddc7b5e5/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black plays ...e6 on move two 17% of the time, and it happened in more than 600,000 games. By playing ...e6, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing ...d5. We will offer a pawn with b4, which is the main idea behind a3.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 60% of the time. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black decides to take on b4 again almost 90% of the time. We'll kick away Black's bishop with c3. The move c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d5 {Black strikes back in the center with ...d5 most frequently, 40% of the time. We'll continue by closing the center with e5. We get a pawn structure that resembles the French, except Black doesn't have the ...c5 break.} 7.e5 7...f6 {Occasionally, Black might try to strike at our center immediately with ...f6, but this simply isn't that great. It happens 12% of the time.} 8.Bd3 {We continue with Bd3 and prepare the powerful Qh5+. White already has a very comfortable advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/9e1c4652-9584-446c-9414-f22bddc7b5e5/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black plays ...e6 on move two 17% of the time, and it happened in more than 600,000 games. By playing ...e6, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing ...d5. We will offer a pawn with b4, which is the main idea behind a3.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 60% of the time. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...d5 {Black can delay recapturing by playing the immediate ...d5, which occurs 4% of the time. We'll respond with e5, closing the center and looking to transpose into the main line. Black might try to continue with ...Nc6, but we can simply reply with d4.} 5.e5 5...Bxb4 {Black captures the pawn on b4 instead, challenging the authority of our gambit. We'll attack Black's bishop with c3 and force it to retreat.} 6.c3 6...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7.} 7.d4 {We continue with d4, transposing into the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/44995511-52a5-4e9a-8bc0-a1a50a3cfdf6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. We will offer up a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the bishop. Let's attack the bishop with c3.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7. This is the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black second option is to set up in a "less confrontational" way with ...d6, and it's played 22% of the time. By playing a small center approach, Black wants to remain solid and claim some space but sacrifices a lot of piece activity for it. Our plan after Bd3 is to set up with f4, Nf3 and O-O, and reply actively to Black's setup.} 7.Bd3 7...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, developing a piece and preparing ...O-O. We expand with f4, grabbing some central space and looking to develop our g1-knight behind the f-pawn.} 8.f4 8...O-O {Black continues by castling their king. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the pawn.} 9.Nf3 9...Nc6 {Black develops their other knight.} 10.O-O {As per usual, the position is objectively equal, but we have great compensation for the sacrificed pawn and Black can easily make a mistake. We'll continue to develop with Nbd2 and look to play a timely e5. If Black pushes in the center, we should avoid trading pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/44995511-52a5-4e9a-8bc0-a1a50a3cfdf6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. We will offer up a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the bishop. Let's attack the bishop with c3.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7. This is the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black second option is to set up in a "less confrontational" way with ...d6, and it's played 22% of the time. By playing a small center approach, Black wants to remain solid and claim some space but sacrifices a lot of piece activity for it. Our plan after Bd3 is to set up with f4, Nf3 and O-O, and reply actively to Black's setup.} 7.Bd3 7...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, developing a piece and preparing ...O-O. We expand with f4, grabbing some central space and looking to develop our g1-knight behind the f-pawn.} 8.f4 8...O-O {Black continues by castling their king. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the pawn.} 9.Nf3 9...b6 {Instead of the natural ...Nc6, Black can also continue with ...b6, looking to quickly fianchetto their light-squared bishop. In this case, we'll postpone castling and strike immediately with e5! Capturing on e5 is actually not advisable for Black, as we can recapture with our f-pawn and play Qc2.} 10.e5 10...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We continue with h4, looking to kickstart an attack on Black's castled king. Black's best response is now ...h6, against which we can look to set up a powerful battery with Bc2 and Qd3.} 11.h4 11...Bb7 {Black continues developing by fianchettoing their bishop instead. What could possibly go wrong?} 12.Bxh7+ {Well, we can strike with the "Greek Gift" sacrifice! After we follow it up with Ng5, Black is simply dead lost! The idea is that the queen is coming to h5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/44995511-52a5-4e9a-8bc0-a1a50a3cfdf6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. We will offer up a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the bishop. Let's attack the bishop with c3.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7. This is the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black second option is to set up in a "less confrontational" way with ...d6, and it's played 22% of the time. By playing a small center approach, Black wants to remain solid and claim some space but sacrifices a lot of piece activity for it. Our plan after Bd3 is to set up with f4, Nf3 and O-O, and reply actively to Black's setup.} 7.Bd3 7...Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, developing a piece and preparing ...O-O. We expand with f4, grabbing some central space and looking to develop our g1-knight behind the f-pawn.} 8.f4 8...Nc6 {This is Black's main alternative to castling. But it tranposes after Nf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 2: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/44995511-52a5-4e9a-8bc0-a1a50a3cfdf6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. We will offer up a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the bishop. Let's attack the bishop with c3.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7. This is the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black second option is to set up in a "less confrontational" way with ...d6, and it's played 22% of the time. By playing a small center approach, Black wants to remain solid and claim some space but sacrifices a lot of piece activity for it. Our plan after Bd3 is to set up with f4, Nf3 and O-O, and reply actively to Black's setup.} 7.Bd3 7...Nc6 {This is Black's main alternative to ...Nf6. But it simply transposes after we continue with f4 and Nf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/7e6d92ef-6726-497e-be33-7ca0bcaef8f2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Quite often, Black plays ...e6 on move two. Let's gambit a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black captures on b4 again. We'll attack the bishop with c3. Still standard.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Retreating to e7. Let's expand in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nf6 {Black's third option is ...Nf6, developing the kingside knight and attacking the e-pawn. It's played almost a quarter of the time. This prompts us to push e5 with tempo and grab even more space.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. Before Black castles, we have the opportunity to slide the queen to g4 and attack the undefended g7-pawn. It's not all that comfortable to defend it.} 8.Qg4 8...Kf8 {Black can also defend the g-pawn with the king.} 9.Qd1 {We'll channel our inner Stockfish by retreating the queen back to d1! This fascinating novelty keeps the queen "flexible" on the central d-file and avoids some annoying ...h5 ideas. We'll look to develop with Nf3, Bd3 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/7e6d92ef-6726-497e-be33-7ca0bcaef8f2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Quite often, Black plays ...e6 on move two. Let's gambit a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black captures on b4 again. We'll attack the bishop with c3. Still standard.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Retreating to e7. Let's expand in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nf6 {Black's third option is ...Nf6, developing the kingside knight and attacking the e-pawn. It's played almost a quarter of the time. This prompts us to push e5 with tempo and grab even more space.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. Before Black castles, we have the opportunity to slide the queen to g4 and attack the undefended g7-pawn. It's not all that comfortable to defend it.} 8.Qg4 8...O-O {Black can also castle, but this is simply too dangerous. Using the pin on the g-file, we move the bishop out to h6 and threaten checkmate.} 9.Bh6 9...g6 {Black defends against checkmate with ...g6.} 10.h4 {Don't rush to capture the f8-rook! Much stronger is h4, threatening to open up Black's king with h5. White has a comfortable advantage, a strong attack and great winning chances.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 3: Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/7e6d92ef-6726-497e-be33-7ca0bcaef8f2/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's continue with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Quite often, Black plays ...e6 on move two. Let's gambit a pawn with b4.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We'll recapture.} 4.axb4 4...Bxb4 {Black captures on b4 again. We'll attack the bishop with c3. Still standard.} 5.c3 5...Be7 {Retreating to e7. Let's expand in the center.} 6.d4 6...Nf6 {Black's third option is ...Nf6, developing the kingside knight and attacking the e-pawn. It's played almost a quarter of the time. This prompts us to push e5 with tempo and grab even more space.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. Before Black castles, we have the opportunity to slide the queen to g4 and attack the undefended g7-pawn. It's not all that comfortable to defend it.} 8.Qg4 8...g6 {Most often, Black defends the g-pawn with ...g6. We can simply play Nf3, supporting the center and looking to develop our light-squared bishop and castle kingside. If Black now castles, we can start an attack on the kingside with h4.} 9.Nf3 9...d6 {Black quickly fights back in the center with ...d6 instead.} 10.Bh6 {We move our bishop out to h6, exposing the weakened dark squares and preventing Black from castling. It is pretty difficult to give a generalized plan, but we should look to develop with Be2 or Bb5 and castle. A good rule of thumb is that we should pin Black's knight if it arrives on c6 or d7.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 4: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/adb1d37d-18d5-431e-b4de-daad136b48f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3.b4 3...d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most often, Black recaptures with the pawn. It happens more than 80% of the time We quickly develop our bishop to b5 and give a check.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Black blocks the check by developing their knight to c6. Surprisingly, this is the only good way to meet the check. We develop our knight to f3 and prepare to castle the king.} 6.Nf3 6...cxb4 {Many players find it tempting to capture the pawn on b4. We castle our king and are now looking to quickly bring our rook to the e-file. It is now inadvisable for Black to capture the a3-pawn, since Re1+ would come with a lot of pressure and Nd4 and c4 ideas.} 7.O-O 7...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We quickly move our rook to e1 and give a check. If Black now blocks the check with the light-squared bishop, we can jump our knight into d4.} 8.Re1+ 8...Be7 {Black blocks the check by moving their dark-squared bishop to e7. We capture the pawn on b4, making use of the fact that both Black's c6-knight and e7-bishop are pinned.} 9.axb4 9...O-O {Black castles their king. This move unpins both of Black's minor pieces and opens up an attack on the b4-pawn. We'll defend and support the b4-pawn with c3.} 10.c3 10...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight.} 11.d4 {We expand in the center with d4. The computer, once again, claims equality, but with all pieces still on the board, and with the structural imbalance on the queenside, the position is still quite "lively". We'll look to continue developing with Bf4 and Nbd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 4: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/adb1d37d-18d5-431e-b4de-daad136b48f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3.b4 3...d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most often, Black recaptures with the pawn. It happens more than 80% of the time We quickly develop our bishop to b5 and give a check.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Black blocks the check by developing their knight to c6. Surprisingly, this is the only good way to meet the check. We develop our knight to f3 and prepare to castle the king.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight now. We'll castle in response, with our rook ready to come to e1 if needed. That in conjuction with Qe2 could be quite strong if Black isn't careful.} 7.O-O 7...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop to e7. Now we can capture on c5, forcing Black to potentially lose a tempo recapturing. And there is simply no reason not to recapture as the simple d4 will secure us a long-term advantage with an extra pawn.} 8.bxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black recaptures the pawn on c5. We begin an initiative by expanding with d4 anyway and attacking Black's bishop.} 9.d4 9...Be7 {Black retreats to e7. We jump our knight into e5, adding an attacker to Black's pinned c6-knight.} 10.Ne5 10...Bd7 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and supports the c6-knight. We will trade for the bishop on d7. This leaves us with the advantageous bishop pair.} 11.Nxd7 11...Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen.} 12.Nd2 {We continue with Nd2, looking to jump our knight to the f3-square. Our rook can develop to e1 and a thematic c4-break is on the cards. If Black plays ...a6 now, we'll capture the knight because of the d4-pawn. Otherwise, we'll look to save the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 4: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/adb1d37d-18d5-431e-b4de-daad136b48f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3.b4 3...d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most often, Black recaptures with the pawn. It happens more than 80% of the time We quickly develop our bishop to b5 and give a check.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {Black can also block the check by developing the bishop to d7. We move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king. This wins another tempo and challenges Black to choose how they will block.} 6.Qe2+ 6...Be7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving their dark-squared bishop to e7. This is, however, inaccurate.} 7.Bb2 {We continue with Bb2, targeting the g7-pawn and immediately punishing Black for moving the bishop from f8! With the combined threats of Bxg7 and bxc5, White has a great initiative and an advantage. Apart from those threats, we also have easy development with Nf3 and O-O. If ...Nf6, we can take.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 4: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/adb1d37d-18d5-431e-b4de-daad136b48f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3.b4 3...d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Most often, Black recaptures with the pawn. It happens more than 80% of the time We quickly develop our bishop to b5 and give a check.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {Black can also block the check by developing the bishop to d7. We move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king. This wins another tempo and challenges Black to choose how they will block.} 6.Qe2+ 6...Qe7 {Black can also block with ...Qe7, offering a trade of queens. We should respond by developing our knight to c3. This move supports the b5-bishop and pressures the d5-pawn.} 7.Nc3 7...Qxe2+ {Black trades the queens on e2. We recapture on e2 with the g-knight. If Black now attempts to capture on b4, Bxd7+ followed by Nxd5 yields White a considerable advantage.} 8.Ngxe2 8...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We proceed by capturing on c5. We didn't want Black to take on b4.} 9.bxc5 9...Bxc5 {Black recaptures on c5.} 10.d3 {White has a cozy advantage after d3, and Black will struggle to defend their isolated d5-pawn for the rest of the game. We can continue developing with O-O and Bg5, looking to further undermine the defense of d5. The natural-looking ...d4 can now conveniently be met with Na4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 4: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/adb1d37d-18d5-431e-b4de-daad136b48f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3.b4 3...d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Black also has the option to recapture with the queen. This is rarer with 13%, but there are still nearly 3,600 games in the database with this position. We develop our queenside knight and attack Black's queen.} 5.Nc3 5...Qe5+ {Most often, Black gives a check by moving the queen to e5. We block the check by developing our bishop to e2. We will look to continue developing with Nf3 and O-O.} 6.Be2 6...cxb4 {In nearly 90% of games which reached the position after Be2, Black made the mistake of capturing the b-pawn. We recapture on b4.} 7.axb4 7...Bxb4 {Black captures the pawn on b4 and simultaneously develops their dark-squared bishop. We quickly jump our knight to f3 and attack Black's queen! If Black now retreats the queen to f6, we can develop our bishop to b2 and line up some nasty discovered attacks.} 8.Nf3 8...Qc7 {Black retreats the queen to c7 instead. We jump our knight into b5 and attack Black's queen once again!} 9.Nb5 9...Qd8 {Black retreats to d8.} 10.Bb2 {We develop our bishop to b2 with what's already a winning advantage. There are two main threats: We are threatening to win the rook with Bxg7, and we are threatening Be5, from where our bishop supports the c7-square for the b5-knight. We might also look to win our pawn back by capturing on a7.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 4: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/adb1d37d-18d5-431e-b4de-daad136b48f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3.b4 3...d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Black also has the option to recapture with the queen. This is rarer with 13%, but there are still nearly 3,600 games in the database with this position. We develop our queenside knight and attack Black's queen.} 5.Nc3 5...Qd8 {Black's other option is to retreat the queen to its home square, d8. We continue by developing our bishop to b2. This is convenient since we've pushed b4.} 6.Bb2 6...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight.} 7.b5 {We close the queenside with b5. White has a slight edge, and we will continue developing with Nf3, g3, Bg2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 4: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/adb1d37d-18d5-431e-b4de-daad136b48f6/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3.b4 3...d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5.} 4.exd5 4...Qxd5 {Black also has the option to recapture with the queen. This is rarer with 13%, but there are still nearly 3,600 games in the database with this position. We develop our queenside knight and attack Black's queen.} 5.Nc3 5...Qd8 {Black's other option is to retreat the queen to its home square, d8. We continue by developing our bishop to b2. This is convenient since we've pushed b4.} 6.Bb2 6...cxb4 {Similarly to the lines with ...Qe5+, Black often makes the mistake of capturing the "poisoned" b-pawn. We recapture on b4.} 7.axb4 7...Bxb4 {Black captures the pawn on b4 and simultaneously develops their dark-squared bishop. What could go wrong this time? Oops! We move our queen out to g4, attacking Black's bishop and the g-pawn.} 8.Qg4 8...Bf8 {Black sets up for the next game by retreating the bishop to f8.} 9.Qg3 {And just as Black thought they were safe, we calmly slide our queen back a square. This seemingly harmless move prepares the deadly Nb5-Nc7, and Black's position is beginning to quickly fall apart. White is simply better and will have a very pleasant game.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 5: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...b6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/6ba58ae4-44d1-4371-87fc-8a3e4590cc8a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll reply with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's strike with b4.} 3.b4 3...b6 {Black declines the gambit with ...b6 8% of the time. This move supports the c-pawn and prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll take on c5. If the bishop recaptures, we can play actively with d4. In general, the opening of the b-file is beneficial for us in these setups.} 4.bxc5 {We continue by capturing on c5.} 4...bxc5 {Black recaptures with the b-pawn instead. We'll simply develop our knight now.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {A close second in popularity is ...Nc6. We develop our bishop to b5, looking to capture on c6 and ruin Black's pawn structure.} 6.Bb5 6...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and supports the c6-knight. We'll castle. If Black now attacks our bishop with ...a6, we can simply retreat it to a4.} 7.O-O 7...Nf6 {Black attacks the e-pawn by developing their kingside knight.} 8.d3 {Remember not to push e5! Instead, we should support the pawn with d3 for a more stable structure. The position is, again, objectively equal, but extremely rich and relatively double-edged. We'll look to finish our development with Bf4 or Bg5, Nbd2 and Re1.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 5: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...b6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/6ba58ae4-44d1-4371-87fc-8a3e4590cc8a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll reply with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's strike with b4.} 3.b4 3...b6 {Black declines the gambit with ...b6 8% of the time. This move supports the c-pawn and prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll take on c5. If the bishop recaptures, we can play actively with d4. In general, the opening of the b-file is beneficial for us in these setups.} 4.bxc5 {We continue by capturing on c5.} 4...bxc5 {Black recaptures with the b-pawn instead. We'll simply develop our knight now.} 5.Nf3 5...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and targets our e-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and defend the attacked pawn.} 6.Nc3 6...Nc6 {Black can also develop the queenside knight.} 7.Bc4 {We'll develop our light-squared bishop to c4. The computer claims equality, but with only the b-pawns having been traded, the position is still extremely interesting. We'll look to finish our development with O-O, d3, Rb1 and Bf4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 5: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...b6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/6ba58ae4-44d1-4371-87fc-8a3e4590cc8a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll reply with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's strike with b4.} 3.b4 3...b6 {Black declines the gambit with ...b6 8% of the time. This move supports the c-pawn and prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll take on c5. If the bishop recaptures, we can play actively with d4. In general, the opening of the b-file is beneficial for us in these setups.} 4.bxc5 {We continue by capturing on c5.} 4...bxc5 {Black recaptures with the b-pawn instead. We'll simply develop our knight now.} 5.Nf3 5...d5 {Black might attempt to immediately strike in the center with ...d5, but this allows us to get a pretty comfortable position. We'll take for starters.} 6.exd5 6...exd5 {Black recaptures on d5. Since Black has moved the d-pawn, we can develop with tempo and give a check with the bishop on b5.} 7.Bb5+ 7...Bd7 {Black blocks the check and offers a trade of bishops. We move our queen to e2, giving a check and supporting the b5-bishop. If Black now blocks the check with their queen, we have the powerful Ne5!} 8.Qe2+ 8...Be7 {Black blocks the check with their bishop instead.} 9.O-O {We castle our king. White now has a pleasant advantage, and we will follow up with Re1, making it difficult for Black to castle. Our queenside bishop can develop to b2, and our queenside knight can develop to c3.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 5: Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...b6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/6ba58ae4-44d1-4371-87fc-8a3e4590cc8a/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll reply with a3.} 2.a3 2...e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's strike with b4.} 3.b4 3...b6 {Black declines the gambit with ...b6 8% of the time. This move supports the c-pawn and prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll take on c5. If the bishop recaptures, we can play actively with d4. In general, the opening of the b-file is beneficial for us in these setups.} 4.bxc5 {We continue by capturing on c5.} 4...bxc5 {Black recaptures with the b-pawn instead. We'll simply develop our knight now.} 5.Nf3 5...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and targets our e-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and defend the attacked pawn.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black attacks the e-pawn once more by developing their kingside knight. In this instance, we can push e5 and counterattack the knight. Pushing d3 would block our light-squared bishop.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. Instead of trading the knights, we can win a tempo on Black's bishop.} 8.Rb1 8...Nxc3 {Before defending or moving the b7-bishop, Black throws in a capture on c3.} 9.dxc3 {We recapture on c3. As per usual, the position is objectively equal, but incredibly dynamic and complex. We'll look to continue developing with Be2, O-O and Bf4 once Black moves away the bishop or defends it.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 / Study 6: Black Plays 2...e5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/4fd625c4-0f9b-4a5c-8f6d-e4bc341ef194/studies/9846e56b-712a-49e6-9413-5684532666d7/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...e5 {You should face ...e5 only 2% of the time. This adds control to the d4-square but weakens the light squares and the d7-pawn severely. ...e5 is actually too weakening and we no longer have to gambit at all! Instead, we'll look to continue in "Vienna" style with Bc4, d3, f4, Nc3 and so on.} 3.Bc4 3...Nc6 {Black continues by developing their queenside knight, and will look to set up with ...Nf6, ...d6 and ...Be7. Regardless of Black's move order, we'll play d3. This move supports the e-pawn, solidifies our center and prepares f4.} 4.d3 4...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight. We continue with our plan by developing the queenside knight.} 5.Nc3 5...Be7 {Black develops their bishop to e7 and prepares to castle kingside.} 6.f4 {We expand with f4 and look to develop our knight to f3. This does weaken our kingside, but Black has no immediate way to expose the weakness of the e1-h4 diagonal. This is now a Two Knights Vienna, with a misplaced c-pawn for Black, and a useful addition of a3 for White. Nf3 and O-O are next.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6, Main Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/a242c163-20ed-485c-a1c7-912888781ceb/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6 on move two 22% of the time, and it happened in more than 750,000 games. Black's 2...d6 controls the e5- square, supports the c-pawn and activates their light-squared bishop. Against this, we will play b4, looking to undermine Black's control of the d4-square.} 3.b4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn 14% of the time. We develop our queenside knight and defend it.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight to c6 and adds an attacker to the b-pawn now. Instead of trying to defend it, we will simply capture on c5.} 5.bxc5 5...dxc5 {Black recaptures on c5.} 6.Bc4 {We develop our light-squared bishop to c4. The open b-file and the questionable placement of Black's c8-bishop for the rest of the game makes our prospects nice. We will look to continue developing with d3, Nf3, O-O and Rb1.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6, Main Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/a242c163-20ed-485c-a1c7-912888781ceb/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6 on move two 22% of the time, and it happened in more than 750,000 games. Black's 2...d6 controls the e5- square, supports the c-pawn and activates their light-squared bishop. Against this, we will play b4, looking to undermine Black's control of the d4-square.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Black can also capture on b4, but this is inadvisable since Black relinquishes control over the important d4-square. This happens almost half the time. We'll respond by recapturing on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We develop our queenside knight and defend the attacked pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...e6 {Black can also look to develop with ...e6 and ...Be7. Against 5...e6, we shouldn't rush with d4, since Black would be able to meet us with a quick ...d5. Instead, we'll prioritize developing our kingside with Nf3, Be2 and castling.} 6.Nf3 6...Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7 and prepares to castle kingside. We continue with our plan by developing the light-squared bishop.} 7.Be2 7...O-O {Black castles their king.} 8.O-O {We castle our king as well. With the space advantage, semi-open a-file and better piece activity, White has an enjoyable advantage. We will look to expand with d4, and our dark-squared bishop can develop to b2 or d2.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6, Main Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/a242c163-20ed-485c-a1c7-912888781ceb/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6 on move two 22% of the time, and it happened in more than 750,000 games. Black's 2...d6 controls the e5- square, supports the c-pawn and activates their light-squared bishop. Against this, we will play b4, looking to undermine Black's control of the d4-square.} 3.b4 3...cxb4 {Black can also capture on b4, but this is inadvisable since Black relinquishes control over the important d4-square. This happens almost half the time. We'll respond by recapturing on b4.} 4.axb4 4...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We develop our queenside knight and defend the attacked pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {Most often, Black looks to set up in "Dragon" style with ...g6 and ...Bg7. We expand in the center with d4, naturally.} 6.d4 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll play h3 in order to prevent any annoying pins with ...Bg4.} 7.h3 7...O-O {Black castles their king.} 8.Be3 {We develop our bishop to e3, supporting the d-pawn. With the space advantage, dominant central presence and the semi-open a-file, White has an advantage. We will look to continue developing with Bd3, Nf3 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6, Main Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/a242c163-20ed-485c-a1c7-912888781ceb/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will respond with a3.} 2.a3 2...d6 {Black plays ...d6 on move two 22% of the time, and it happened in more than 750,000 games. Black's 2...d6 controls the e5- square, supports the c-pawn and activates their light-squared bishop. Against this, we will play b4, looking to undermine Black's control of the d4-square.} 3.b4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn 14% of the time. We develop our queenside knight and defend it.} 4.Nc3 4...g6 {Black chooses the fianchetto approach. We will slide our rook to b1 prophylactically. Also, we are planning to open up the b-file.} 5.Rb1 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll continue by capturing on c5 and opening up that b-file. Our rook is coming to light now since it's harder for Black's bishop to develop.} 6.bxc5 6...dxc5 {Black recaptures on c5.} 7.Bc4 {This position is very similar to the main continuation, and the same idea applies. We will look to continue developing with Nge2, O-O and d3. The important nuance is that we should develop our kingside knight to e2 (and not f3), in order to support the c3-knight, and to be able to meet any ...Bg4 with f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 2: Black Pushes The Early 2...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/f2ff579b-d0eb-4541-ba12-f3e94d76ced3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2.a3 2...d5 {Black chooses to strike in the center with ...d5. This happens 4% of the time. No reason not to capture and leave Black with a strong center.} 3.exd5 3...Nf6 {Some players try to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the pawn with 3...Nf6. It occurs 20% of the time. We will try to develop rapidly as well by giving a check on b5 with the bishop.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Nbd7 {Black can also block the check with the b8-knight. We'll develop our queenside knight and defend the d-pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...a6 {Expect to see ...a6 at some point. We'll retreat to e2.} 6.Be2 6...Nb6 {Black jumps the knight into b6, opening up a triple attack on the weak d-pawn, which looks problematic. We'll develop our knight to f3, though. We cannot hold onto the pawn, so we'll look to gain a significant lead in development while Black spends time winning it back.} 7.Nf3 7...Nbxd5 {Black captures on d5, restoring material balance. Let's trade once.} 8.Nxd5 8...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen.} 9.O-O {We castle our king. With a clear lead in development, White now simply has a better position. Our plan is to expand in the center and on the queenside with c4, d4 and b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 2: Black Pushes The Early 2...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/f2ff579b-d0eb-4541-ba12-f3e94d76ced3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2.a3 2...d5 {Black chooses to strike in the center with ...d5. This happens 4% of the time. No reason not to capture and leave Black with a strong center.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 77% of the time. This is now the Scandinavian Defense, but with the inclusion of a3 and c5. We don't have to rush with Nc3 and can develop to f3 first.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. Let's develop with tempo on the queen now.} 5.Nc3 5...Qd8 {Black goes back to d8. We can continue in an aggressive fashion, this time activating the bishop to b5. We are threatening to capture and disrupt Black's pawn structure.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd7 {That's why Black develops the bishop to d7 and supports the c6-knight. How can we continue being aggressive? We can jump to e4 and attack Black's c5-pawn now!} 7.Ne4 7...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and defends the pawn.} 8.c3 {We'll continue with c3, supporting the b4- and d4-squares. This was another idea behind Ne4. White has a slight edge, and we'll look to continue with O-O and d4 or even b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 2: Black Pushes The Early 2...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/f2ff579b-d0eb-4541-ba12-f3e94d76ced3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2.a3 2...d5 {Black chooses to strike in the center with ...d5. This happens 4% of the time. No reason not to capture and leave Black with a strong center.} 3.exd5 3...Nf6 {Some players try to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the pawn with 3...Nf6. It occurs 20% of the time. We will try to develop rapidly as well by giving a check on b5 with the bishop.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by developing the bishop to d7. There is little merit to retreating here, so we'll trade.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen. There are two attackers on the pawn now. The only way to save it is c4.} 6.c4 6...e6 {Black plays ...e6, looking to chip away at our pawn wedge. That's why we will slide the queen to e2 and pin Black's e6-pawn. The queen is now more active and participates in defending our pawn structure.} 7.Qe2 7...Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7, unpinning the pawn and preparing to castle kingside. We have to capture on e6 at this point.} 8.dxe6 8...fxe6 {Black recaptures with the pawn as to keep the queens on the board. This is another reason behind Qe2; if Black doesn't want to trade queens down a pawn, they have to worsen their pawn structure!} 9.Nf3 {We'll simply develop our knight to f3. With an extra pawn and a clear target on e6, White is simply better and will have a relatively easy game. We can look to continue developing with O-O, d3 and Nc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 2: Black Pushes The Early 2...d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/f2ff579b-d0eb-4541-ba12-f3e94d76ced3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2.a3 2...d5 {Black chooses to strike in the center with ...d5. This happens 4% of the time. No reason not to capture and leave Black with a strong center.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 77% of the time. This is now the Scandinavian Defense, but with the inclusion of a3 and c5. We don't have to rush with Nc3 and can develop to f3 first.} 4.Nf3 4...Nf6 {Black often develops the kingside knight, but this is inaccurate! We can take the initiative by developing our knight to c3 and attacking Black's queen.} 5.Nc3 5...Qd8 {Black retreats the queen to its home square, d8.} 6.d4 {We strike in the center with d4! White now has a pleasant, active position with easy development. We will look to develop with Be3, and in many cases Qe2 and O-O-O. If Black captures on d4, we should recapture with the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 3: Black Plays  2...b6 or 2...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/60378ec2-0585-4db9-a0a6-db321bda172b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2.a3 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn 2% of the time. It's logical to push e5 and counterattack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll strike in the center with d4! We don't have to play b4 like usual here.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We recapture on d4 and attack Black's knight.} 5.Qxd4 5...e6 {Black plays ...e6, defending the knight and opening up the f8- bishop. We develop our knight to f3 in a standard manner.} 6.Nf3 6...Nc6 {Black will attack our queen with ...Nc6 at some point. We'll retreat the queen to its home square, d1. From there it's safe and still has potential.} 7.Qd1 7...d6 {By far the most common is ...d6, since Black is looking to get rid of our e-pawn. But we won't play it slow and give Black what they want. We start an initiative by attacking Black's knight with c4! If Black now retreats the knight to c7, we can simply continue with Bf4.} 8.c4 8...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight to b6 instead. We'll attack Black's queen with Bg5 now! If Black now carelessly plays ...Be7, we can simply win a pawn after Bxe7 and exd6.} 9.Bg5 9...Qc7 {So, Black moves the queen to c7. Let's capture the pawn on d6 and finally relieve the tension.} 10.exd6 10...Bxd6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll develop our knight to c3, threatening the deadly Nb5.} 11.Nc3 11...a6 {Black prevents Nb5 with ...a6. But we'll attack Black's bishop by jumping our knight into e4 instead!} 12.Ne4 12...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7. The initiative doesn't stop! We attack the b6-knight with c5.} 13.c5 13...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5, naturally. We will trade the bishops on e7. If Black recaptures with the queen, we can jump our knight into d6.} 14.Bxe7 14...Kxe7 {Black recaptures with the king instead.} 15.Be2 {We'll develop our bishop to e2, preparing to castle kingside. The c5-pawn is a thorn in Black's position, the d6-square is a potential outpost for our knight, and Black's king on e7 is misplaced. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 3: Black Plays  2...b6 or 2...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/60378ec2-0585-4db9-a0a6-db321bda172b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2.a3 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn 2% of the time. It's logical to push e5 and counterattack the knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll strike in the center with d4! We don't have to play b4 like usual here.} 4.d4 4...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We recapture on d4 and attack Black's knight.} 5.Qxd4 5...e6 {Black plays ...e6, defending the knight and opening up the f8- bishop. We develop our knight to f3 in a standard manner.} 6.Nf3 6...Nc6 {Black will attack our queen with ...Nc6 at some point. We'll retreat the queen to its home square, d1. From there it's safe and still has potential.} 7.Qd1 7...Qc7 {Black moves the queen to c7 and adds an attacker to the e5-pawn. We attack Black's knight with c4! The line that follows is pretty much completely forced. If Black now retreats the knight to b6, we can simply continue with Bf4.} 8.c4 8...Nde7 {Black retreats the knight to e7. It's important to develop the knight to c3 and ignore the attack on the e-pawn. You will see why.} 9.Nc3 9...Nxe5 {Black captures the pawn on e5. We can jump our knight into b5, attacking Black's queen.} 10.Nb5 10...Nxf3+ {Black captures on f3 with check. We'll recapture with the queen.} 11.Qxf3 11...Qe5+ {Black moves the queen to e5 and gives a check to our king. This is probably what you will face. We block the check by moving our light-squared bishop to e2. We are now threatening the devastating Bf4.} 12.Be2 12...Ng6 {Black jumps the knight into g6, defending against the threat. We slide our queen to g3, forcing a queen trade. Black cannot decline the trade, since our queen and knight coordinate towards the c7-square!} 13.Qg3 13...Qxg3 {Black trades queens on g3. We recapture with the h-pawn. We are now threatening Nc7+.} 14.hxg3 14...Kd8 {Black defends c7 with the king.} 15.Bd2 {We are down a pawn for the moment, but Black's position is simply dreadful. Not only are we likely to win the pawn back, but our pieces are also super active and we have a significant lead in development. According to the engine, Black's only try is now ...b6, to which we will respond with Bf3 and Nxa7, winning our pawn back.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 / Study 3: Black Plays  2...b6 or 2...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/8d12cef8-ec7a-421e-b7f5-19798e4255fc/chapters/bf4f5270-f105-4c0a-8df0-a9a2e4279aa9/studies/60378ec2-0585-4db9-a0a6-db321bda172b/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black goes for 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2.a3 2...b6 {Black plays ...b6 only 1% of the time, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and supporting the c5-pawn. We don't really have to go b4 now. Instead, we'll quickly strike in the center with d4!} 3.d4 3...cxd4 {Black captures on d4. We'll recapture on d4 with our queen.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Black develops the knight to c6 and attacks our queen, which is expected. We slide the queen back to d3. Wasting this tempo is not the end of the world, since Black has committed to b7 with the bishop, which is a bad square.} 5.Qd3 5...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the light-squared bishop. It's not all that active, though. We'll simply develop our knight to c3.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight.} 7.Nf3 {We develop our other knight to f3. The dominant central presence and Black's misplaced light-squared bishop give White a pleasant edge. We'll look to develop our dark-squared bishop next and castle queenside.} *